WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:02.544
(jazz music)

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:17.642
(engine revving)

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:26.109
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:30.613
- [Announcer] The new hospital
marks notable a advance

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:32.991
in Negro hospitalization.

00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:35.368
A field which I regret to say

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:39.456
has been sadly neglected in
many sections of our country.

00:00:40.915 --> 00:00:44.294
There have been 11 PWA Hospital
Projects for Negro patients

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:47.047
including Homer G. Phillips

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.798
which I am told is the largest

00:00:48.923 --> 00:00:51.551
and finest of its kind in the
world.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.636
The new building will
give the Negros a chance

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:57.474
to assert themselves in
medicine and surgery.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:01:00.560
The fact that the splendid
structure

00:01:00.643 --> 00:01:03.063
has been dedicated to the Negro
race,

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:06.232
is definite proof of the
friendly feeling

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:08.735
and the lessening of racial
prejudice,

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:12.155
now existing in this great city
and state.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:19.370
Its real significance is that
at once and the same time,

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:21.539
it symbolizes the just demands

00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:24.084
and needs of our Negro citizens,

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:27.337
and the willingness of the
part of those in authority,

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:32.175
to help achieve your rightful
place in our economic system.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.012
- [Narrator] To understand
why the grand opening

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:40.183
of Homer G. Phillips Hospital
was such a significant event

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:42.185
you first need to know a little
bit

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.938
about the history of St. Louis.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:47.524
Homer G. Phillips Hospital is
proof

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:52.278
of how the excellence of
a few affected so many.

00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:54.072
- When you look at the
history of St. Louis,

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:56.074
around 1905 through 1930,

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:02.247
it was a time when a lot
of those organizations,

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.958
civic organizations were
fighting.

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.711
But it wasn't just in St. Louis
it was all over the country.

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:11.214
Is what I call a 100 years

00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:13.758
of emancipation without freedom.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:19.639
You know, emancipated in
1865, and people had to fight

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:25.228
all the way to 1965 before
all those basic freedoms

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:28.398
and given the same kind
of rights and benefits

00:02:28.523 --> 00:02:30.150
that everybody else has.

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:32.819
- We were in the Jim Crow era in
Missouri,

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:37.073
which, you know, certainly
is a border state, right?

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:39.576
So you know, we aren't
in the deep South,

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:41.244
but certainly we're sort of
still thinking

00:02:41.369 --> 00:02:45.915
about black folks were not
treated equitably here, right?

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.625
Like it was sort of, the vision
was to get

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:49.169
across the river to Illinois,

00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:51.462
and that was a sort of the true
North.

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:54.966
- St. Louis was certainly one
of the most segregated cities

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.011
in the United States after the
civil war,

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:01.472
because of the black
migration from the South,

00:03:01.556 --> 00:03:05.768
that took place and a lot
of blacks moved north,

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:07.353
along the Mississippi river

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:10.190
and St. Louis was one of
the stopping off points.

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:12.025
One of the important things was
that

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:13.818
St. Louis was so segregated

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:17.614
because the blacks were
in one area of St. Louis,

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:20.825
Italians in the other
and Germans on the other.

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:23.786
- Well The Ville was on
the outskirts of St. Louis.

00:03:23.912 --> 00:03:26.956
It originally was not part of
the city.

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:29.667
And it belonged to a white
florist

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:34.214
and he had a race track, and he
had an inn

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:38.885
where people would stop off,
you know, in Ellettsville

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:40.845
at first, that's the original
name.

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:44.474
And it was shortened
over time to The Ville.

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:48.019
- So The Ville was actually,
it was a stable community.

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:49.062
It was very stable,

00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:52.315
very well known African
American community.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.067
And it was the site of the first

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.152
African American high school

00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.155
west of Mississippi, Sumner.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.367
- We had Poro College, which
was one of the first schools

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.495
for the training of African
American women

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.330
in the beauty culture business.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:10.583
Chuck Berry also was a student
at that school I understand.

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:11.960
- [Robin] Annie Malone Home,

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:14.545
she was one of the first
black female entrepreneurs

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:15.672
in the United States.

00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:18.341
Created a foster care
home for black children.

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:21.219
- We had Turner School
for handicap youngsters,

00:04:21.302 --> 00:04:25.223
built in 1925, the
first school of its kind

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.767
for African American youngsters
in the United States.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.853
I think one important
thing to remember is that

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:35.858
many of the people who worked
in The Ville lived The Ville.

00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:40.196
And so you had a climate
where you had the workers,

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:43.574
plus the professionals all
intermingling,

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:45.368
attending church together,

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:47.537
seeing each other on a daily
basis,

00:04:47.662 --> 00:04:49.706
which made it a special place to
live.

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:53.710
- Nowadays, they always talk
about a walkable community,

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:57.880
The Ville was the original
walkable community.

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:00.383
We had everything we
needed in our community.

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:03.261
So we're talking about a
segregated neighborhood,

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:07.890
but the key was that we had a
diverse population of people

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:10.393
in there from different income
levels.

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:14.314
We could walk anywhere, to the
store, we had grocery stores,

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:17.275
we had fresh fruit and vegetable
stands,

00:05:17.358 --> 00:05:20.945
we even had a movie theater in
the Poro.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:22.905
- The neighborhoods were
black neighborhoods.

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:25.074
There was the black
doctor, the black lawyer,

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:26.784
the black administrators,

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:29.120
folks lived in the same
neighborhood.

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:32.332
So it gave us an opportunity
to see professionals,

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:35.752
to see tradesmen, to see
business owners,

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:38.087
actually interact in
their own neighborhood.

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:40.173
We had these role models that
were there,

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:43.426
certainly was segregated,
certainly as Missouri,

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:45.595
certainly it's just north
of the Mason-Dixon line.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:46.971
But at the end of the day,

00:05:47.055 --> 00:05:50.641
we had an opportunity to
just be kids growing up

00:05:50.725 --> 00:05:52.685
in a community that looked like
us.

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:55.355
- That's almost nonexistent
today

00:05:57.106 --> 00:06:00.735
because what we've gone is
we've gone to the malls.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.154
So when you go to the malls,

00:06:03.237 --> 00:06:07.492
people are coming from
neighborhoods they don't live in

00:06:07.575 --> 00:06:12.663
and getting service
whereas when I was a kid,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.832
didn't have unemployment, why?

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:17.919
Because all of the stores and
things

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:21.714
that was in your community
had to hire people

00:06:21.798 --> 00:06:24.717
who lived in the community
to service these people.

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:27.053
(jazz music)

00:06:29.764 --> 00:06:33.351
- The Ville being less than a
square mile,

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:37.480
it made it a community
where people felt connected,

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:41.692
Money stayed in the
community a number of times

00:06:41.776 --> 00:06:43.277
before it disappeared.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:44.695
I think that's important to
remember

00:06:44.779 --> 00:06:47.073
because you build wealth in a
community

00:06:47.156 --> 00:06:50.368
by wealth staying in the
community.

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:54.372
And I think another thing,
it's good to remember,

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:58.709
it thrived because others
couldn't.

00:06:58.793 --> 00:07:00.211
And let me explain that,

00:07:00.294 --> 00:07:03.297
many of the teachers and
professionals

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:07.552
were forced by segregation
to work in the community.

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:12.223
So many of the people who
lived here got the best

00:07:12.306 --> 00:07:14.642
because they couldn't get the
best.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:18.813
Another thing that
happened during that time,

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:21.816
teachers were not allowed to get
married.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:24.402
So many of the students
became their family.

00:07:24.485 --> 00:07:28.072
- Doctors, they couldn't
get married on that time.

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:31.117
And my uncle really who became
medical director later on,

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.079
married my aunt Dorothy,
who was a school teacher,

00:07:35.163 --> 00:07:37.748
but they had to keep it
hidden during the time.

00:07:37.832 --> 00:07:40.084
They were married for
the first four years.

00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:42.462
It was just thought the
doctors couldn't perform

00:07:42.545 --> 00:07:44.630
their duties if they were
married early

00:07:44.714 --> 00:07:47.425
or school teachers, were thought
that they couldn't perform

00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:50.678
as a school teacher if they had
a husband.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.763
I don't know why the social
structure was like that,

00:07:52.847 --> 00:07:54.432
but that's the way it was.

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:58.436
- Teachers, doctors, lawyers,
businessmen,

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:02.315
prominent businessmen,
all lived in The Ville.

00:08:02.398 --> 00:08:05.610
It was a thriving community
with people making money,

00:08:05.693 --> 00:08:08.779
and most of the stores and
all the business places

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:10.448
were owned by black people.

00:08:10.531 --> 00:08:12.700
This was great to live in The
Ville.

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:14.869
We made movie there, Poro Hotel.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:18.956
Annie Malone, Annie Malone, so
Annie started the Poro Hotel.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.335
And when you wanted to be an
attorney,

00:08:22.418 --> 00:08:25.880
the only place that you could
go was Lincoln University

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:29.467
and Lincoln did not have a law
school

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.678
so they started the law
school in the Poro Hotel,

00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:36.849
and black attorneys taught
young men to be attorneys.

00:08:36.933 --> 00:08:40.311
So this all happened at the
Poro Hotel in The Ville.

00:08:40.394 --> 00:08:43.105
- I went to Sumner High
School and across street

00:08:43.231 --> 00:08:47.193
from my high school was
the teacher's college.

00:08:47.276 --> 00:08:49.070
Around the corner

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:53.991
was the branch of Lincoln
University's Law School.

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:57.662
So we saw students who
were becoming lawyers.

00:08:57.745 --> 00:09:01.040
So what I saw as a high school
student

00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:06.003
is us are the extension of
education that let me know that

00:09:06.087 --> 00:09:08.923
where I was going to high
school four years later

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:12.760
was not the end of my
educational system,

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.428
but it was the springboard

00:09:14.554 --> 00:09:17.598
because I saw other
people going to college,

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.850
working on a PhD, that looked
good to me.

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:23.229
They looked like champions
of the community.

00:09:23.312 --> 00:09:25.648
(tense music)

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:28.901
- Attorney Homer G. Phillips

00:09:28.985 --> 00:09:32.113
is sort of an enigmatic
historical figure.

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:36.867
Many people know certain
elements of his life,

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:39.704
but there's a lot more
that hasn't been discovered

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:41.706
about attorney Homer G.
Phillips.

00:09:41.789 --> 00:09:46.544
He was born John Wesley
Phillips, I do have evidence.

00:09:46.627 --> 00:09:50.548
There's a small annotation in a
newspaper

00:09:50.631 --> 00:09:53.301
from Sedalia around 1899,

00:09:53.426 --> 00:09:56.262
that announces that a John
Wesley Phillips

00:09:56.345 --> 00:10:00.141
has changed his name to Homer G.
Phillips,

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.268
Homer Garland Phillips.

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:04.812
I think that sort of name change

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:08.566
preceded his matriculation to
Howard University Law School.

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:10.109
Many people don't know that

00:10:10.192 --> 00:10:12.486
attorney Homer G. Phillips
was a staunch advocate

00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:15.823
for civil rights, black civil
rights.

00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:18.409
He was a staunch advocate
for black voting,

00:10:18.492 --> 00:10:20.911
he was a staunch Republican
activist,

00:10:20.995 --> 00:10:24.165
he helped get President
Herbert Hoover elected

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.334
in the Midwest region.

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:30.171
He was also a staunch
advocate for equal rights,

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:34.175
and particularly working
against sort of rising

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.844
segregation policies in the
city.

00:10:36.969 --> 00:10:39.138
So Homer G. really fought
against the implementation

00:10:39.221 --> 00:10:42.475
of Deed Restrictive
Covenants, for instance.

00:10:42.558 --> 00:10:44.268
Homer G. actually was
instrumental

00:10:44.352 --> 00:10:49.190
in securing over $200,000 in
relief for those black families

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:54.111
who were affected in the East
St. Louis Race Riot in 1917.

00:10:54.195 --> 00:10:57.782
And that itself really
sort of catapulted him to

00:10:57.865 --> 00:10:59.116
not only the local recognition,

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:01.327
but also national recognition.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.788
- Very, very popular man in St.
Louis.

00:11:03.871 --> 00:11:06.415
They wanted him even to run for
mayor,

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.709
the city of St. Louis during
those times.

00:11:08.834 --> 00:11:11.629
And I think if he ran, he was
probably,

00:11:11.712 --> 00:11:12.880
probably he would have won

00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:14.298
becoming the first African
American mayor

00:11:14.382 --> 00:11:17.551
in the City of St. Louis in the
1930s.

00:11:17.677 --> 00:11:21.055
- Homer G. Phillips was one of
the many,

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.642
many African Americans whose
parents

00:11:24.725 --> 00:11:26.727
came out of the civil war,

00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:32.692
got a taste of freedom in
times of the reconstruction,

00:11:32.775 --> 00:11:35.486
when the 13, 14 and 15
amendments of the constitution

00:11:35.569 --> 00:11:40.533
were passed, abolished
slavery, granted citizenship

00:11:40.616 --> 00:11:44.662
and call for equal
treatment of all citizens.

00:11:44.745 --> 00:11:48.833
The opportunity to build a black
family,

00:11:48.916 --> 00:11:51.210
became a lot more or realistic

00:11:52.628 --> 00:11:54.422
with the end of slavery.

00:11:54.547 --> 00:11:57.341
Because one of the major
aspects of slavery,

00:11:57.425 --> 00:12:00.177
is they made a precise attack

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:04.724
on that basic unit of social
organization, the black family,

00:12:04.807 --> 00:12:07.518
where you sell the child
this way, the mother that way

00:12:07.601 --> 00:12:09.228
and the father that way,

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:12.189
you create an incredible
crisis for those family

00:12:12.273 --> 00:12:15.693
from which we have never fully
recovered.

00:12:15.776 --> 00:12:20.489
So you have to see Homer G.
Phillips as one of the products

00:12:21.657 --> 00:12:24.618
that came out of that
reconstruction era.

00:12:24.744 --> 00:12:27.788
- He was an attorney, a
very prominent attorney,

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:30.416
who had a strong sense of social
justice.

00:12:30.499 --> 00:12:34.253
And he had lent his name and his
expertise

00:12:34.336 --> 00:12:37.757
to a number of projects
in the neighborhood

00:12:39.592 --> 00:12:42.219
of uplifting in the community.

00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:44.138
He had a particular interest in
health,

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:47.224
and I'm not so sure why,
but this was a subject

00:12:47.308 --> 00:12:50.227
that just really caught his
attention.

00:12:50.311 --> 00:12:52.104
- Not only was Homer G. a
lawyer,

00:12:52.188 --> 00:12:56.567
he was also involved in
distributing low interest loans

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:59.403
to African American community
members

00:12:59.487 --> 00:13:02.072
and he was also a part of
the newspaper business.

00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:03.616
So he was a part of the
newspaper business

00:13:03.699 --> 00:13:06.118
in Sedalia, Missouri, his
hometown.

00:13:06.202 --> 00:13:10.456
But he also was a partner in
newspapers here in St. Louis,

00:13:10.539 --> 00:13:11.832
so particularly the St. Louis
Argus,

00:13:11.957 --> 00:13:15.336
which was one of the first
African American newspapers

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:16.629
in St. Louis.

00:13:16.712 --> 00:13:18.297
But then he began to sort of
switch

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.424
and began to work with judge
Nathan Young

00:13:20.508 --> 00:13:24.261
later on in his life when he
realized

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:25.971
that there's a sort of massive
transition

00:13:26.055 --> 00:13:27.515
happening among black voters,

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.475
namely that black voters were
switching

00:13:29.558 --> 00:13:32.645
from the Republican Party to
the Democratic Party, right?

00:13:32.728 --> 00:13:35.272
And so the St. Louis Argus
and the St. Louis American,

00:13:35.356 --> 00:13:37.608
and Homer G.'s interaction
with both of those,

00:13:37.691 --> 00:13:39.819
actually mirrors that sort of
transition.

00:13:39.902 --> 00:13:42.279
- The newspaper business
was extremely relevant

00:13:42.363 --> 00:13:44.323
in the African American
community earlier,

00:13:44.406 --> 00:13:46.033
particularly when we
had this great migration

00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:47.326
from the South.

00:13:47.409 --> 00:13:49.203
I was always interested in
public policy

00:13:49.328 --> 00:13:51.997
because I thought that had
an impact on people's lives.

00:13:52.081 --> 00:13:55.125
And I thought that
having a black newspaper

00:13:55.209 --> 00:13:57.336
would enable me to have some
impact.

00:13:57.419 --> 00:13:59.380
National Trade Association

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:03.300
has called us the number
one black newspaper

00:14:03.384 --> 00:14:05.469
in the country in the last six
years,

00:14:05.553 --> 00:14:07.972
with the largest weekly
newspaper of any kind

00:14:08.055 --> 00:14:09.640
in the State of Missouri.

00:14:09.723 --> 00:14:14.395
So I'm very proud of what has
happened with an institution

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.981
that was started in 1928.

00:14:17.064 --> 00:14:19.400
Homer Phillips was a lawyer and
activist

00:14:19.525 --> 00:14:20.985
who we admire a great deal here,

00:14:21.068 --> 00:14:22.486
was a part of the original
ownership

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.071
of the St. Louis American.

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:27.157
And I would say Homer Phillips,

00:14:27.241 --> 00:14:30.077
the person who was held in
high esteem by his community,

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:33.664
we've tried to use that
as sort of an example,

00:14:33.747 --> 00:14:37.668
a model for how we should
conduct ourselves as a
newspaper.

00:14:37.751 --> 00:14:41.338
- He was also very active in
getting the African Americans

00:14:41.422 --> 00:14:44.091
elected to public office.

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:48.888
He supported a bond issue,
$8 million bond issue,

00:14:48.971 --> 00:14:51.390
with the understanding that $1
million

00:14:51.473 --> 00:14:54.894
would go for construction of a
hospital

00:14:54.977 --> 00:14:59.023
for African Americans that would
be run by African Americans

00:14:59.106 --> 00:15:00.733
and operated by them.

00:15:00.816 --> 00:15:04.653
He wanted it to be a
community control hospital.

00:15:06.113 --> 00:15:08.532
And he wanted it to be in the
African American community

00:15:08.616 --> 00:15:12.119
- City Beautiful bond by mayor
Henry Keel,

00:15:12.244 --> 00:15:14.371
was a massive infrastructure
plan,

00:15:14.455 --> 00:15:17.583
over $80 million to improve
city infrastructure,

00:15:17.666 --> 00:15:20.377
to provide new buildings for the
city

00:15:20.461 --> 00:15:22.880
and that's the time when
you have black doctors

00:15:22.963 --> 00:15:26.091
who are beginning to say
they needed a place to train.

00:15:26.175 --> 00:15:27.301
They needed a place

00:15:27.426 --> 00:15:29.887
where they could sharpen
their medical skills,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.389
where they could get access to
patients

00:15:32.473 --> 00:15:33.933
and where they could really
become

00:15:34.016 --> 00:15:35.559
higher echelon physicians,

00:15:35.643 --> 00:15:37.645
meaning specialized physicians

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:40.898
which requires extra years of
training.

00:15:40.981 --> 00:15:42.024
Originally, Homer G. Phillips

00:15:42.149 --> 00:15:44.401
was actually opposed to
a segregated hospital.

00:15:44.485 --> 00:15:48.238
He did not advocate for
any type of segregation

00:15:48.322 --> 00:15:51.241
and that included segregation in
hospitals

00:15:51.325 --> 00:15:53.118
which sort of goes against kind
of

00:15:53.202 --> 00:15:55.454
the popular understanding
of Homer G., right?

00:15:55.537 --> 00:15:57.623
That he led the movement to get

00:15:57.706 --> 00:15:59.750
Homer G. Phillips Hospital
started.

00:15:59.833 --> 00:16:01.418
That eventually happened.

00:16:01.502 --> 00:16:04.171
But initially Homer G. actually

00:16:04.296 --> 00:16:06.423
would have preferred that
the two City Hospitals

00:16:06.507 --> 00:16:08.592
in St. Louis actually be
integrated.

00:16:08.676 --> 00:16:09.843
But when the city sort of said

00:16:09.969 --> 00:16:11.303
we're going to stick with
segregation

00:16:11.387 --> 00:16:13.973
against segregation policies
that are being implemented

00:16:14.056 --> 00:16:17.518
at this exact same time that
were not there before, right?

00:16:17.643 --> 00:16:20.187
This is due in part to
sort of black migration

00:16:20.312 --> 00:16:21.647
from the rural South.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.608
I mean people are coming through
St. Louis stopping there.

00:16:24.692 --> 00:16:27.945
So the black population
is expanding rapidly

00:16:28.028 --> 00:16:30.823
and the old City Hospital
for African American,

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:34.326
City Hospital number two, was
not enough,

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:35.786
- And this was people's hospital

00:16:35.869 --> 00:16:38.205
that was totally inadequate.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.208
And also they put blacks in
the City Hospital number one

00:16:41.333 --> 00:16:44.628
in the basement behind the steam
pipes.

00:16:44.712 --> 00:16:47.381
So because of the poor care
that blacks were getting there,

00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:50.634
because St. Louis had a
large influx of blacks

00:16:50.718 --> 00:16:53.345
from the South after slavery,

00:16:53.429 --> 00:16:54.972
Homer G. Phillips thought
that this would be

00:16:55.055 --> 00:16:58.017
the best thing to do to have a
hospital

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:01.520
totally built for the black
community.

00:17:01.603 --> 00:17:03.981
- And so Homer G. was
instrumental in getting

00:17:04.064 --> 00:17:07.067
a key provision in the 1923 bond
issue

00:17:08.402 --> 00:17:09.987
for a Negro hospital.

00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:12.906
There was debate about
whether that newer hospital

00:17:13.032 --> 00:17:15.743
should be separate from
City Hospital number one,

00:17:15.868 --> 00:17:18.579
if it should be close to
City Hospital number one.

00:17:18.704 --> 00:17:19.413
- There were those who said

00:17:19.538 --> 00:17:21.832
let's put it as an attachment,

00:17:21.915 --> 00:17:26.670
an annex to the white hospital
under white administration.

00:17:26.754 --> 00:17:28.172
He said no.

00:17:28.255 --> 00:17:31.341
- And so Homer G. said
instead, and many agreed,

00:17:31.425 --> 00:17:34.511
that if they weren't gonna have
a hospital

00:17:34.595 --> 00:17:36.930
where they could practice
autonomously,

00:17:37.056 --> 00:17:39.433
doctors and nurses could
practice autonomously

00:17:39.558 --> 00:17:41.935
whereby patients could
have dignity, you know,

00:17:42.061 --> 00:17:45.564
not to suffer the sort of
indignities of white physicians,

00:17:45.647 --> 00:17:48.108
then they needed a
completely separate hospital.

00:17:48.233 --> 00:17:51.528
- He went to the board of
aldermen

00:17:51.612 --> 00:17:53.197
where it was finally decided

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.574
that it would be a separate
hospital

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:57.785
because they wanted to
renege on that at first.

00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.995
- And that debate took 10 years.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:04.124
So from the 1923 bond issue to
1933,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:06.376
ground is initially being broken

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:09.213
on the Homer G. Phillips
Hospital.

00:18:09.296 --> 00:18:12.716
- And all through that
time Jim Crow was raging.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:14.968
(R&amp;B music)

00:18:20.307 --> 00:18:22.893
St. Louis City was extremely
segregated.

00:18:22.976 --> 00:18:26.396
I'm talking about most black
folks were concentrated

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:29.733
east on the other side
of Jefferson Avenue.

00:18:29.817 --> 00:18:31.902
Then it was Grant, then it was
King side

00:18:31.985 --> 00:18:33.821
before they started expanding
West.

00:18:33.946 --> 00:18:34.988
But the important point is

00:18:35.114 --> 00:18:38.492
that shortly after the banish
was passed

00:18:38.617 --> 00:18:41.245
Homer G. Phillips was
assassinated

00:18:42.371 --> 00:18:45.124
right there on Delmar and
Euclid.

00:18:45.207 --> 00:18:49.753
- It's unfortunate that he
didn't really live to see

00:18:49.837 --> 00:18:51.296
his dream come to fruition,

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.800
because under mysterious
circumstances

00:18:54.883 --> 00:18:57.594
he was shot by two gentlemen

00:18:57.678 --> 00:19:01.598
on his way to his office on a
street car.

00:19:01.682 --> 00:19:03.934
A murder which was
never solved by the way,

00:19:04.017 --> 00:19:06.103
- Debates, you know, vary,
again,

00:19:06.186 --> 00:19:09.022
according to why Homer G. was
murdered.

00:19:09.148 --> 00:19:12.151
It seemed initially
that the two individuals

00:19:12.234 --> 00:19:16.280
who were seen, witnessed
shooting Homer G. Phillips

00:19:16.363 --> 00:19:20.450
shot him because of debates
about an estate check

00:19:20.534 --> 00:19:24.288
that Homer G. would not
disburse to his client.

00:19:24.371 --> 00:19:26.707
There was a dispute about
whether

00:19:26.832 --> 00:19:30.878
the daughter of a man or
his sort of younger wife

00:19:31.003 --> 00:19:33.881
would get the estate and
it was a sizeable estate,

00:19:34.006 --> 00:19:36.133
I believe it was around $3,000.

00:19:36.216 --> 00:19:38.177
Homer G. successfully secured
the money

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.345
for the daughter of this man,

00:19:40.429 --> 00:19:43.390
but he charged $1,000 for his
fees.

00:19:43.515 --> 00:19:44.892
They did not pay that fee.

00:19:45.017 --> 00:19:48.145
And so it was assumed
that Homer G. was murdered

00:19:48.228 --> 00:19:51.815
because he did not disburse
the whole of their check.

00:19:51.899 --> 00:19:53.192
Two individuals were caught.

00:19:53.275 --> 00:19:54.860
They were apprehended,

00:19:54.943 --> 00:19:56.653
they were identified in lineups.

00:19:56.737 --> 00:19:58.363
They were even brought to trial,

00:19:58.447 --> 00:19:59.990
but neither were convicted of
the crime.

00:20:00.073 --> 00:20:02.910
It's strange to me at least
and it's strange other people

00:20:03.035 --> 00:20:05.245
that two young African American
men

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.916
who were witnessed
publicly shooting this man

00:20:10.083 --> 00:20:11.710
were not convicted of the crime.

00:20:11.793 --> 00:20:13.712
That's interesting to me.

00:20:14.922 --> 00:20:17.507
- Of course the killer
was never apprehended

00:20:17.591 --> 00:20:19.218
and of course, you know, if
you don't look for people,

00:20:19.301 --> 00:20:20.636
you don't find them.

00:20:20.761 --> 00:20:25.015
And I'm sure in those days, in
the circumstances behind it,

00:20:25.098 --> 00:20:27.559
there was not a lot of looking

00:20:27.643 --> 00:20:29.895
because it would have taken
effort to find

00:20:29.978 --> 00:20:33.023
and that was not high
on the priority scale

00:20:33.106 --> 00:20:35.442
as we would say now to
find out who killed him.

00:20:35.567 --> 00:20:36.860
- I don't know either way.

00:20:36.944 --> 00:20:40.030
I don't think there is
gonna be a definitive answer

00:20:40.113 --> 00:20:43.700
as to why Homer G. was
assassinated, murdered,

00:20:43.784 --> 00:20:45.410
however you wanna sort of
describe that.

00:20:45.494 --> 00:20:49.206
But it is curious to me
that these two young men

00:20:49.289 --> 00:20:52.918
who had arrest records before
this, right?

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:54.795
Were not convicted of the crime.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:55.629
Were they paid?

00:20:55.754 --> 00:20:56.463
No one knows.

00:20:56.588 --> 00:20:57.714
I mean, you know, was this a
hit?

00:20:57.798 --> 00:20:58.757
No one knows.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:00.884
Was this really about the estate
check?

00:21:00.968 --> 00:21:01.885
No one knows.

00:21:02.844 --> 00:21:04.554
Because that case
was not closed,

00:21:04.638 --> 00:21:06.223
I mean that really sort of paved
the way

00:21:06.306 --> 00:21:08.725
to the institution itself being
named

00:21:08.809 --> 00:21:10.477
after Homer G. Phillips.

00:21:10.602 --> 00:21:14.815
- It is the number one most
prominent unsolved murder

00:21:16.692 --> 00:21:20.404
in the history of the city of
St. Louis.

00:21:20.487 --> 00:21:24.783
He paid a price for this
facility, paid with his life.

00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:28.120
He's a kind of martyr that you
have to put

00:21:28.203 --> 00:21:31.581
on the same level as Martin
Luther King,

00:21:31.665 --> 00:21:33.917
you know, Malcolm X.,

00:21:34.001 --> 00:21:35.669
and Homer G. Phillips.

00:21:37.129 --> 00:21:40.590
Even though we don't speak
that much enough about him,

00:21:40.674 --> 00:21:43.760
you will not find any monument
anywhere

00:21:43.844 --> 00:21:46.263
outside of this facility

00:21:46.346 --> 00:21:48.807
to the memory or to the
incredible contribution

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:50.642
of attorney Homer G. Phillips.

00:21:50.726 --> 00:21:52.144
- Calls were immediately made

00:21:52.227 --> 00:21:53.854
to name the hospital after
Homer G.

00:21:53.979 --> 00:21:55.647
So right after his death, right?

00:21:55.731 --> 00:21:59.943
And it seemed relevant to put
his name on this hospital.

00:22:00.027 --> 00:22:02.154
But you do have individuals
like David Grant

00:22:02.237 --> 00:22:05.198
who would later say naming
Homer G. Phillips Hospital

00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:08.660
is actually a sort of
a double edged sword.

00:22:08.744 --> 00:22:11.997
On the one end, it is certainly
an institution of pride.

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:13.373
It's a vision of progress,

00:22:13.498 --> 00:22:15.625
particularly in this
neighborhood.

00:22:15.709 --> 00:22:18.962
On the other hand, it still
was a segregated institution,

00:22:19.046 --> 00:22:20.714
so even though the history of
segregation

00:22:20.839 --> 00:22:24.885
has produced this sort of
jewel in history as it were,

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:26.303
you know, it's still a
segregated institution

00:22:26.386 --> 00:22:28.722
and Homer G. fought
every type of segregation

00:22:28.847 --> 00:22:29.890
tooth and nail,

00:22:30.015 --> 00:22:34.311
and I wonder, you know,
were Homer G. alive today,

00:22:34.394 --> 00:22:35.645
you know, what would he really
think

00:22:35.729 --> 00:22:37.647
about his name being on this
institution?

00:22:37.731 --> 00:22:42.235
- That was supposed to be
Colored Hospital Number Two

00:22:42.361 --> 00:22:45.906
and of course, you know,
it was always number two.

00:22:46.031 --> 00:22:50.911
I came from that era and I
understand one, two, A, B.

00:22:51.036 --> 00:22:53.830
So I do not call those
hospitals.

00:22:53.914 --> 00:22:55.165
I call them by their name.

00:22:55.248 --> 00:22:58.377
I called City Hospital, not
City Hospital Number One,

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:02.381
but it was either City Hospital
or it was Max Starkloff

00:23:02.464 --> 00:23:03.757
and Homer Phillips' hospital

00:23:03.882 --> 00:23:08.387
was Homer G. Phillips Hospital
not City Hospital Number Two.

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:09.679
- Both sorta sentiments are
there.

00:23:09.763 --> 00:23:11.932
They exist simultaneously.

00:23:12.057 --> 00:23:14.851
It is important that we sort of
remember

00:23:14.935 --> 00:23:17.854
this black lawyer at
this black institution,

00:23:17.938 --> 00:23:19.231
but at the same time,

00:23:19.314 --> 00:23:21.525
the circumstances that
produced this institution

00:23:21.608 --> 00:23:23.693
themselves are deplorable.

00:23:23.777 --> 00:23:25.946
(R&amp;B music)

00:23:27.114 --> 00:23:29.950
- When Homer G. Phillips opened
up,

00:23:30.075 --> 00:23:31.785
it was a great day of
celebration.

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:36.206
Thousands filled the streets,
all dignitaries came,

00:23:36.289 --> 00:23:38.875
the newspapers toasted as one of
the best

00:23:38.959 --> 00:23:43.088
African American hospitals
in the community,

00:23:43.171 --> 00:23:45.048
St. Louis and the world.

00:23:45.132 --> 00:23:47.092
- Again, you have to
remember the time also.

00:23:47.175 --> 00:23:49.261
This is 1937.

00:23:49.344 --> 00:23:53.890
And this is a time when
when Roosevelt himself

00:23:53.974 --> 00:23:56.768
is very anxious about the war
effort

00:23:58.145 --> 00:24:01.106
and really reaching out to the
African American community.

00:24:01.189 --> 00:24:03.400
Frankly, it may not
have been the president

00:24:03.483 --> 00:24:06.236
as much as it was Eleanor
Roosevelt

00:24:06.319 --> 00:24:10.157
who really was the driving
force for social change,

00:24:10.282 --> 00:24:14.911
and so she perhaps was the
one who moved Harold Ickes,

00:24:14.995 --> 00:24:16.788
who was secretary of the
interior

00:24:16.872 --> 00:24:20.000
to support the Homer G. Phillips
Hospital.

00:24:20.125 --> 00:24:22.961
And in fact, he was
here during the program

00:24:23.044 --> 00:24:27.257
during the opening of the
hospital opening ceremony.

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:29.426
So it really was a national
event.

00:24:29.509 --> 00:24:32.095
- The mayor of St. Louis
was there, Bernard Dickman.

00:24:32.179 --> 00:24:33.263
But what's interesting about him

00:24:33.346 --> 00:24:35.599
is that he actually sort
of, before he became mayor,

00:24:35.682 --> 00:24:37.767
was involved in the St.
Louis real estate exchange,

00:24:37.851 --> 00:24:39.436
which was heavily involved

00:24:39.519 --> 00:24:42.230
in restricting housing in St.
Louis

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:45.609
and steering black and white
individuals

00:24:45.692 --> 00:24:47.319
to different parts of the town.

00:24:47.402 --> 00:24:51.948
So even though the mayor
celebrated this hospital

00:24:52.032 --> 00:24:53.366
it's sort of also celebrated too

00:24:53.492 --> 00:24:55.827
that he was able to build this
hospital

00:24:55.911 --> 00:24:59.956
as opposed to his Republican
predecessor, Victor Miller.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:01.041
The hospital project languished

00:25:01.166 --> 00:25:03.293
for a decade under his
administration.

00:25:03.376 --> 00:25:05.879
So in many ways, even though
Dickman

00:25:06.004 --> 00:25:08.215
had this sort of strange
history,

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:10.133
I mean to sort of secure the
black vote,

00:25:10.217 --> 00:25:12.469
which is really important in St.
Louis,

00:25:12.552 --> 00:25:13.887
I mean he really sort of put his
efforts

00:25:14.012 --> 00:25:15.472
to building this hospital.

00:25:15.555 --> 00:25:18.308
You also had the governor,
you had state representatives,

00:25:18.391 --> 00:25:20.977
and again, this also sort of
embodies

00:25:21.061 --> 00:25:23.563
a sort of a new deal moment,
right?

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:25.148
You had these sort of, you know,

00:25:25.232 --> 00:25:26.816
the public works administration,

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:29.152
the works progress
administration
were deeply involved

00:25:29.236 --> 00:25:31.988
in helping to fund and
construct this hospital.

00:25:32.072 --> 00:25:35.700
So the infusion of that money
allowed it to really become

00:25:35.784 --> 00:25:37.160
one of the biggest black
hospitals

00:25:37.244 --> 00:25:39.079
in the nation to be quite frank.

00:25:39.204 --> 00:25:41.331
- Well, Homer G. Phillips
was very close to me

00:25:41.414 --> 00:25:44.584
because my father was in
the first group of interns

00:25:44.709 --> 00:25:47.003
to train in Homer G. Phillips.

00:25:47.087 --> 00:25:52.008
The story goes that in 1937
when the hospital was built,

00:25:52.092 --> 00:25:54.678
my father graduated from
Meharry Medical College

00:25:54.761 --> 00:25:57.055
in Nashville, Tennessee

00:25:57.138 --> 00:26:00.684
and he and 27 of his fellow
classmates

00:26:00.767 --> 00:26:02.852
were on their way to Kansas City

00:26:02.936 --> 00:26:05.855
to intern the Kansas City
General.

00:26:05.939 --> 00:26:08.858
They had to change trains in St.
Louis

00:26:08.942 --> 00:26:10.569
and while they were in the train
stations,

00:26:10.652 --> 00:26:13.405
a red cap, which was the colored
porters

00:26:13.488 --> 00:26:15.240
that put bags on a train,

00:26:15.323 --> 00:26:17.617
asked them where they were
going.

00:26:17.742 --> 00:26:19.411
They said, well, we're
going to Kansas City.

00:26:19.494 --> 00:26:21.538
He said, well, they just
built a colored hospital here

00:26:21.621 --> 00:26:23.873
and they don't have any doctors.

00:26:23.957 --> 00:26:27.627
So they went out on a taxi
and checked at the hospital

00:26:27.752 --> 00:26:28.962
and they all decided to stay

00:26:29.087 --> 00:26:31.965
since it weren't bad with $10 a
month,

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:33.383
clothes and rolling board.

00:26:33.466 --> 00:26:34.551
And that was the same thing

00:26:34.634 --> 00:26:36.720
that was going on in Kansas
city.

00:26:36.803 --> 00:26:41.600
So they became the first 27
interns at Homer G. Phillips.

00:26:41.683 --> 00:26:43.435
- This was large.

00:26:43.518 --> 00:26:45.729
Homer G. Phillips wasn't just a
hospital,

00:26:45.812 --> 00:26:47.814
it was gonna be one of the
greatest

00:26:47.939 --> 00:26:50.275
African American hospitals in
the country.

00:26:50.358 --> 00:26:54.613
We had Hubbard hospital
and we had Howard hospital

00:26:54.696 --> 00:26:56.072
and there were smaller
hospitals,

00:26:56.156 --> 00:26:59.784
but nothing of this stature
had ever been conceptualized

00:26:59.868 --> 00:27:02.829
or built for the African
American community

00:27:02.954 --> 00:27:05.582
and so this was a huge
undertaking.

00:27:05.665 --> 00:27:09.252
I think that just really was,
you know,

00:27:09.336 --> 00:27:12.005
illustration of Homer
G. Phillips the attorney

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:14.174
of his broader vision
that he was gonna create

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:17.093
this momentous remarkable
hospital,

00:27:17.177 --> 00:27:19.763
which was gonna be the training
program

00:27:19.846 --> 00:27:24.434
for at least a third of all
African American physicians

00:27:24.517 --> 00:27:26.811
between 1937 and 1979.

00:27:26.895 --> 00:27:29.272
Just a remarkable feat in
itself.

00:27:29.356 --> 00:27:33.109
Then we had a nurses
program, nursing school,

00:27:33.193 --> 00:27:35.195
and there's a tunnel that
goes from a nursing school

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:36.988
all the way to the hospital

00:27:37.072 --> 00:27:38.657
where you didn't have to go
outside.

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:42.160
- And because the facility was
so special,

00:27:42.243 --> 00:27:44.954
they built the main hospital,

00:27:45.038 --> 00:27:47.123
a nursing home and a power
plant,

00:27:47.207 --> 00:27:49.542
and also an interns quarters.

00:27:49.668 --> 00:27:52.504
The nursing home was built for
148 nurses

00:27:52.587 --> 00:27:56.925
and the interns was housed,
I think it was 28 interns.

00:27:58.218 --> 00:28:01.888
The hospital was built
to take care of 635 beds,

00:28:03.014 --> 00:28:03.973
which was really something,

00:28:04.057 --> 00:28:06.184
it was one of the largest
hospitals

00:28:06.267 --> 00:28:07.686
in the country at the time.

00:28:07.769 --> 00:28:11.147
- Again, although The
Ville was there beforehand,

00:28:11.231 --> 00:28:13.650
Homer G. Phillips really put his
stamp,

00:28:13.733 --> 00:28:15.402
his imprimatur on The Ville

00:28:15.527 --> 00:28:19.030
and the two really were
just indistinguishable.

00:28:19.114 --> 00:28:22.992
Homer G. was The Ville
and The Ville was Homer G.

00:28:23.076 --> 00:28:25.370
- So by the time of the
hospital's dedication

00:28:25.453 --> 00:28:29.416
the hospital was named
Homer G. Phillips Hospital

00:28:29.541 --> 00:28:31.668
and the dedication ceremony,

00:28:31.751 --> 00:28:33.753
one of the last items on the
program

00:28:33.878 --> 00:28:36.423
was that city officials

00:28:36.548 --> 00:28:38.675
handed the keys of a hospital

00:28:38.758 --> 00:28:42.011
to the hospital's medical
director, Henry Hampton,

00:28:42.095 --> 00:28:44.347
who was the first African
American

00:28:44.431 --> 00:28:46.391
medical director of the
hospital.

00:28:46.474 --> 00:28:47.934
- This is at the time of course
now

00:28:48.059 --> 00:28:49.894
when during rigid segregation

00:28:49.978 --> 00:28:52.522
in large parts of the country,

00:28:52.605 --> 00:28:54.441
and it's fallacy that African
Americans

00:28:54.566 --> 00:28:56.860
could not run a hospital.

00:28:56.943 --> 00:28:59.195
African American doctors
could not deliver care

00:28:59.279 --> 00:29:01.197
of the quality of whites.

00:29:01.281 --> 00:29:03.032
Physicians, African American
nurses

00:29:03.116 --> 00:29:05.910
could not deliver care the
way white nurses could.

00:29:05.994 --> 00:29:09.080
And so Harry Hampton had this
knowledge

00:29:10.665 --> 00:29:14.794
and he had every desire
to break that stereotype,

00:29:14.919 --> 00:29:17.213
to produce a hospital

00:29:17.297 --> 00:29:20.133
which was gonna be of the
highest quality

00:29:20.258 --> 00:29:24.471
having the highest volume and
really creating innovation,

00:29:24.596 --> 00:29:27.432
which will be adopted by white
hospitals.

00:29:27.515 --> 00:29:30.977
In the first year from 1937 to
1938,

00:29:31.102 --> 00:29:34.439
they had over 100,000 patients
visit.

00:29:34.522 --> 00:29:36.733
Now The Ville neighborhood
is relatively small,

00:29:36.816 --> 00:29:37.901
as you can imagine.

00:29:37.984 --> 00:29:39.569
So you can see this is drawing
patients

00:29:39.652 --> 00:29:41.780
from a large catchment area.

00:29:41.863 --> 00:29:45.325
And Harry Hampton said, when
they walked through this door,

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:48.244
America's eyes are on us.

00:29:48.328 --> 00:29:49.788
We're gonna show that we can do
this

00:29:49.871 --> 00:29:51.915
and do this extraordinarily well

00:29:51.998 --> 00:29:54.250
and this is what Henry Hampton
wanted,

00:29:54.334 --> 00:29:55.502
this was his legacy.

00:29:55.627 --> 00:29:57.253
He wanted people to remember
this

00:29:57.337 --> 00:30:00.089
as a phenomenally great
hospital.

00:30:00.173 --> 00:30:02.091
Not a phenomenally great black
hospital,

00:30:02.175 --> 00:30:04.427
but a great hospital period.

00:30:04.511 --> 00:30:06.679
- Then Dr. William Sinkler took
over

00:30:06.805 --> 00:30:09.849
who was a sort of prolific
figure

00:30:09.974 --> 00:30:11.267
in the history of the hospital

00:30:11.351 --> 00:30:15.605
and really not only had
a hand in making sure

00:30:15.688 --> 00:30:17.524
that the hospital itself ran
smoothly,

00:30:17.649 --> 00:30:20.777
but also really helped to
develop

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:23.446
the internship and residency
programs.

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:26.491
- And it was through the
program that he set up,

00:30:26.574 --> 00:30:30.286
that knowledge, it came from
Sinkler

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:32.664
and his knowhow and whatever

00:30:32.747 --> 00:30:36.668
to all of the modalities
of general surgery.

00:30:36.751 --> 00:30:40.505
And I was a privileged
to have a scrubbed with him

00:30:42.674 --> 00:30:44.509
on several occasions.

00:30:44.592 --> 00:30:46.344
In doing so you learn,

00:30:46.427 --> 00:30:50.181
you watch his technique, how
he approached the problem,

00:30:50.265 --> 00:30:51.891
what he did with the problem

00:30:52.016 --> 00:30:53.810
and the outcome that he had

00:30:53.893 --> 00:30:57.146
and all of it was very, very
admirable.

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:01.860
- You know, he was a
surgeon, and I was a pedi,

00:31:02.735 --> 00:31:04.863
and we were on the fifth floor,

00:31:06.364 --> 00:31:09.993
and they were on the second
or third floor I think.

00:31:10.076 --> 00:31:12.287
And of course he had us over,

00:31:13.913 --> 00:31:15.540
usually you didn't see him
unless

00:31:15.623 --> 00:31:18.710
you were getting called on to
carve it,

00:31:21.546 --> 00:31:23.423
first I knew of it.

00:31:23.548 --> 00:31:24.549
(laughter)

00:31:24.632 --> 00:31:26.676
- Well Dr. William H.
Sinkler was a classmate

00:31:26.759 --> 00:31:29.053
of my father at Howard
university,

00:31:29.137 --> 00:31:30.930
so they knew each other well.

00:31:31.055 --> 00:31:34.392
He was small in stature,
he was about five foot six

00:31:34.475 --> 00:31:37.437
and they called him little
Willy because he was so short,

00:31:37.562 --> 00:31:39.856
but he had a step up on a
stool when they operated.

00:31:39.939 --> 00:31:42.901
But Dr. Sinkler was a whizz at
surgery

00:31:42.984 --> 00:31:46.946
so because of the poor care
that they had down South,

00:31:47.071 --> 00:31:51.367
one of the pharmacists
down there, Dr. Pierce,

00:31:51.451 --> 00:31:53.912
asked Dr. Sinkler when
he come down and operate

00:31:53.995 --> 00:31:57.373
on some poorly taken care
of patients in Monroe.

00:31:57.457 --> 00:32:00.126
So because it was a Catholic
hospital

00:32:00.251 --> 00:32:01.628
and because it was segregated,

00:32:01.753 --> 00:32:04.297
they wouldn't take or
operate on any patients

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:07.050
in Monroe that were black.

00:32:07.133 --> 00:32:09.427
So Dr. Sinkler and Dr. Vaughn

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:11.304
went down to Monroe, Louisiana

00:32:11.429 --> 00:32:13.723
to operate on these patients

00:32:13.806 --> 00:32:15.600
that had been selected for them.

00:32:15.683 --> 00:32:17.060
So when they get to Monroe,

00:32:17.143 --> 00:32:20.313
they had to change the clothes
in the janitor's closet.

00:32:20.438 --> 00:32:21.981
So when they went in the
operating room,

00:32:22.106 --> 00:32:23.650
since they had several patients,

00:32:23.775 --> 00:32:25.068
some of the patients needed

00:32:25.151 --> 00:32:27.737
more than one operation to be
done.

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.448
One particular lady needed a
hysterectomy

00:32:30.531 --> 00:32:32.116
and a gallbladder taken out,

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:35.244
which was unusual to do at the
same time,

00:32:35.328 --> 00:32:38.331
so you'd expect the operation
to take a long time.

00:32:38.456 --> 00:32:41.417
The white doctors in the
community gathered around a
table

00:32:41.501 --> 00:32:43.920
to watch these doctors operate,

00:32:44.003 --> 00:32:48.091
and because they had to be
operating pretty quickly,

00:32:48.174 --> 00:32:49.592
they was just whizzing along

00:32:49.676 --> 00:32:50.760
and the one of the white doctors

00:32:50.843 --> 00:32:52.178
just couldn't contain himself
and said,

00:32:52.303 --> 00:32:55.515
"Ooh-wee, look at these
little niggers go,"

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.101
and so that was a story that
just went around the hospital

00:32:58.184 --> 00:33:01.604
and everybody just laughed
about how fast Dr. Sinkler was.

00:33:01.688 --> 00:33:03.106
- When you think of, you know,

00:33:03.189 --> 00:33:06.442
the legion of African American
physicians

00:33:06.526 --> 00:33:10.113
from James Whittico to LaSalle
Leffall

00:33:10.196 --> 00:33:12.949
who became president, the first
African American president

00:33:13.032 --> 00:33:15.451
of the American Cancer Society.

00:33:15.535 --> 00:33:18.496
These people are world renowned

00:33:18.579 --> 00:33:20.456
and they were here at Homer G.

00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:25.003
because there was this
sense of greater purpose.

00:33:25.086 --> 00:33:28.297
We had to get the best to train
the best.

00:33:28.381 --> 00:33:30.800
- [James] There was only three
places

00:33:30.883 --> 00:33:34.012
in the United States where Negro
doctors

00:33:35.179 --> 00:33:38.558
can get a qualified internship

00:33:39.684 --> 00:33:41.728
and those three places were

00:33:43.896 --> 00:33:47.233
Howard University, Washington
D.C.,

00:33:47.358 --> 00:33:49.652
Meharry Medical College,

00:33:49.736 --> 00:33:53.197
and then came Homer Phillips
Hospital,

00:33:53.281 --> 00:33:56.200
and that was, fortunately
enough, the exception.

00:33:56.284 --> 00:33:58.036
- Well, one of my favorite
stories was James Whittico

00:33:58.119 --> 00:34:02.290
came to complete his
internship at Homer G. Phillips

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:06.544
and so he said when he drove his
car in,

00:34:06.627 --> 00:34:09.005
he looked up and he saw
the hospital, he said.

00:34:09.088 --> 00:34:10.882
- [James] I couldn't believe it,

00:34:10.965 --> 00:34:14.469
I'd never seen a structure
that magnificent,

00:34:15.928 --> 00:34:19.057
that large, and that new

00:34:20.433 --> 00:34:24.020
and so I parked and just checked
it

00:34:24.103 --> 00:34:26.898
and looked at this beautiful
hospital.

00:34:26.981 --> 00:34:29.901
And I was just carried
away with

00:34:31.027 --> 00:34:33.905
this great structure that ain't
never seen

00:34:33.988 --> 00:34:36.240
anything like it before in my
life.

00:34:36.324 --> 00:34:38.409
- Everyone who walked in
had that same feeling.

00:34:38.493 --> 00:34:41.204
It just, it really was
breathtaking

00:34:41.287 --> 00:34:42.747
the way it was structured,

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:46.959
the beauty of this hospital
and people wanted to be here.

00:34:47.085 --> 00:34:51.214
- My sister, my daughter's
aunt, was a pediatrician.

00:34:52.507 --> 00:34:54.467
She was there first

00:34:54.592 --> 00:34:58.304
and she also trained at
Homer Phillips Hospital

00:35:00.014 --> 00:35:02.934
and she was the main reason I
came.

00:35:03.017 --> 00:35:06.312
- Dr. Helen Nash was a very
strong woman,

00:35:06.437 --> 00:35:08.981
physician, pediatrician here in
St. Louis,

00:35:09.107 --> 00:35:10.483
well known, very competent.

00:35:10.608 --> 00:35:14.904
- My aunt Helen, her time spent
at Homer Phillips Hospital,

00:35:14.987 --> 00:35:18.324
she was one of few women
residents there at the hospital

00:35:18.449 --> 00:35:20.576
and I think in her internship
class,

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:23.621
maybe she was one of five or six
women

00:35:23.704 --> 00:35:28.251
and she then did a residency
in pediatrics there

00:35:28.334 --> 00:35:31.629
and one of her big
interests was in the care

00:35:31.712 --> 00:35:36.425
of premature infants and
she did a lot to improve

00:35:36.509 --> 00:35:39.679
the conditions in the nursery
at Homer Phillips Hospital

00:35:39.804 --> 00:35:42.932
to have them kind of up to
the standard of nurseries,

00:35:43.015 --> 00:35:45.101
you know, in other hospitals
across the country.

00:35:45.184 --> 00:35:47.937
- I was on pediatrics with Dr.
Helen Nash

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:51.440
and as stated before,
everybody was afraid of her,

00:35:51.524 --> 00:35:53.609
but she was excellent.

00:35:53.693 --> 00:35:57.155
I was feeding a baby, laying on
his back

00:35:57.238 --> 00:36:00.575
and she walked in and she told
me I was drowning the baby.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:04.662
I have never fed a baby
laying flat on its back again.

00:36:04.745 --> 00:36:08.708
I always sit it up straight
and I will never forget that.

00:36:08.833 --> 00:36:10.626
- [Helen] Well, everybody came
to Homer G.

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:11.961
It was one of the best places.

00:36:12.044 --> 00:36:14.630
They took the largest number of
interns

00:36:14.714 --> 00:36:17.466
and you could be sure
of getting

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:20.678
a residency.

00:36:20.761 --> 00:36:23.472
Pediatrics, that's what I wanted
to do

00:36:23.556 --> 00:36:27.393
and so the major thing
was to get accepted here.

00:36:28.895 --> 00:36:31.063
- One of the most
qualified women to finish

00:36:31.189 --> 00:36:34.025
from Homer G. Phillips was Helen
Nash,

00:36:34.108 --> 00:36:35.693
who was a pediatrician.

00:36:35.776 --> 00:36:36.986
She and her brother Homer

00:36:37.069 --> 00:36:41.073
both ran a practice of
pediatrics for at least 50 years

00:36:41.199 --> 00:36:43.242
in the city of St. Louis.

00:36:43.367 --> 00:36:44.744
She was one of the first blacks

00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:49.832
to be on the staff of pediatrics
in St. Louis University.

00:36:49.916 --> 00:36:54.045
Took care of the indigent
in St. Louis for years

00:36:54.128 --> 00:36:57.340
and to this day I think
she was one of the best

00:36:57.423 --> 00:37:01.177
pediatricians that I ever worked
with being an obstetrician.

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:03.763
I delivered the babies
that she took care of.

00:37:03.888 --> 00:37:06.849
- So Helen Nash was a force of
nature.

00:37:07.767 --> 00:37:09.060
She was a force of nature

00:37:09.143 --> 00:37:11.062
because she had a chip on her
shoulder.

00:37:12.438 --> 00:37:14.690
Her father said, "Helen, I
don't want you to be a doctor.

00:37:14.774 --> 00:37:17.777
I want you to do something in
the arts."

00:37:17.902 --> 00:37:21.572
She said, "Wait a minute, oh,
I'm a girl

00:37:21.656 --> 00:37:24.200
and you don't want me to be
a doctor because I'm a female.

00:37:24.283 --> 00:37:27.119
I'm not gonna buy that," and so
she said,

00:37:27.245 --> 00:37:28.204
"No, I'm gonna be a doctor

00:37:28.287 --> 00:37:30.206
whether you want me
to be a doctor or not."

00:37:30.289 --> 00:37:31.707
And so she carried that,

00:37:31.791 --> 00:37:33.709
she had something to prove to
herself,

00:37:33.793 --> 00:37:35.711
you know, in a misogynistic
society,

00:37:35.795 --> 00:37:37.380
she's gonna show that she's not
gonna be

00:37:37.463 --> 00:37:39.382
just a doctor, but the doctor.

00:37:39.465 --> 00:37:41.425
- Helen Nash is a legend
in this community.

00:37:41.509 --> 00:37:43.552
I mean, I think she represented
maybe

00:37:43.636 --> 00:37:45.388
second or third generation.

00:37:45.471 --> 00:37:49.225
Her brother, her niece,

00:37:49.976 --> 00:37:51.644
her dad were physicians

00:37:52.645 --> 00:37:55.564
and everybody who knows her
career

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:58.484
holds her in the highest esteem.

00:37:58.609 --> 00:38:00.069
She was a great pioneer.

00:38:00.152 --> 00:38:03.990
She's one of several who
practice medicine

00:38:04.115 --> 00:38:06.951
at a very high level and was
welcomed

00:38:07.034 --> 00:38:11.414
into the more broad stream
of academic medicine.

00:38:11.497 --> 00:38:17.753
- So Helen had a reputation
for being really, really feisty

00:38:18.796 --> 00:38:22.425
and really, I used to say, you
know,

00:38:22.508 --> 00:38:25.094
she didn't take any prisoners,
but what I meant by that

00:38:25.177 --> 00:38:29.015
was that she was, you
know, it was her way,

00:38:29.140 --> 00:38:31.767
and she stood up for what
she believed was right

00:38:31.851 --> 00:38:33.519
and she was not gonna let anyone
tell her

00:38:33.644 --> 00:38:36.647
she couldn't do what she
knew she was capable of

00:38:36.731 --> 00:38:38.983
or should be allowed to
do, that sort of thing.

00:38:39.066 --> 00:38:41.319
- She was incredibly
good with her patients,

00:38:41.402 --> 00:38:44.447
her small patients or babies
and with the family members,

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:46.782
the things that she
did in order to improve

00:38:46.866 --> 00:38:47.992
maternal child health.

00:38:48.075 --> 00:38:49.618
- [Helen] Infant mortality rate,

00:38:49.702 --> 00:38:51.871
morbidity and mortality
rates were very high

00:38:51.996 --> 00:38:54.457
for infants all over the country

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:57.460
and the premature
infants at Homer Phillips

00:38:57.543 --> 00:39:00.629
because there was no set up
prem-y unit

00:39:02.381 --> 00:39:06.469
at the time had a death rate
above 80%.

00:39:06.552 --> 00:39:08.137
- And she noticed that, well,

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:12.683
why are all these babies,
you know, in the same bed,

00:39:12.767 --> 00:39:14.310
same crib when one's sneezing

00:39:14.393 --> 00:39:16.645
and one could have the croup?

00:39:16.729 --> 00:39:18.356
We should have separate
bassinets

00:39:18.439 --> 00:39:20.524
for these kids, for these
babies.

00:39:20.608 --> 00:39:22.151
Now you will say that, well,

00:39:22.234 --> 00:39:24.737
that's pretty, you know,
obvious,

00:39:25.946 --> 00:39:27.990
there's nothing sophisticated
about that.

00:39:28.074 --> 00:39:30.576
It was, that was a novel
concept

00:39:30.701 --> 00:39:32.078
and she did something simple

00:39:32.203 --> 00:39:34.372
as to have individual bassinets.

00:39:34.455 --> 00:39:36.749
The infection rates plummeted.

00:39:36.874 --> 00:39:38.084
- Because of the groundwork

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:40.836
she laid because she had done so
well,

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:43.381
it made it easier for me to come

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:47.343
because I did not have to prove
things

00:39:47.426 --> 00:39:51.180
that she had already proven
and they just sort of expected,

00:39:51.263 --> 00:39:53.599
oh, this was another
person doing the same thing

00:39:53.724 --> 00:39:56.685
and she was of course a
teacher and a mentor for me.

00:39:56.769 --> 00:39:59.522
- One of the things that
was really important

00:39:59.605 --> 00:40:03.526
for Helen is that her
patients were treated well.

00:40:04.944 --> 00:40:07.113
- [Helen] I went to the diet
kitchen

00:40:07.238 --> 00:40:09.865
and I asked the dietician

00:40:09.949 --> 00:40:12.034
who had gotten to be a friend of
mine

00:40:12.118 --> 00:40:15.913
why we never had ice cream
and we never had bananas

00:40:15.996 --> 00:40:18.874
and she said, I will
show you the grocery list

00:40:18.958 --> 00:40:21.585
that came from downtown,

00:40:21.669 --> 00:40:23.879
and ice cream and bananas were
marked

00:40:23.963 --> 00:40:26.966
by City Hospital only, the white
hospital.

00:40:27.091 --> 00:40:28.884
I went to the medical director

00:40:28.968 --> 00:40:31.637
and to the superintendent
who was McKnight,

00:40:31.762 --> 00:40:33.931
the medical director was Dr.
Sinkler,

00:40:34.014 --> 00:40:36.976
I said, "Can you imagine
that children on my ward

00:40:37.101 --> 00:40:39.228
can't have ice cream
because they are black?"

00:40:39.311 --> 00:40:42.857
He will say, "Miss Helen,
here you go again."

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:46.735
I said, "You have to have
ice cream and bananas

00:40:46.819 --> 00:40:48.154
if you wanna look right."

00:40:48.279 --> 00:40:49.655
Then they started to alternate.

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:52.408
Then they cause so much ruckus

00:40:52.491 --> 00:40:55.453
that they just said everybody
have ice cream every day,

00:40:55.536 --> 00:40:57.329
all you want, so forget it.

00:40:57.455 --> 00:40:58.497
- Helen, Helen,

00:41:01.834 --> 00:41:02.960
when she made up her mind,

00:41:03.043 --> 00:41:04.420
you couldn't change it.

00:41:04.503 --> 00:41:06.672
- She was the first
African American female

00:41:06.797 --> 00:41:08.591
to join the staff at Washington
University

00:41:08.674 --> 00:41:10.426
at the children's hospital.

00:41:10.509 --> 00:41:12.761
- [Helen] I was in the new
newborn unit

00:41:12.845 --> 00:41:14.597
scrubbed up in cap and gown

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:17.641
and I'm getting ready to
go into the newborn unit

00:41:17.725 --> 00:41:21.729
to look at a baby and this
nurse came in and said,

00:41:23.189 --> 00:41:25.941
"Oh, you're getting ready
to examine your grand baby."

00:41:26.025 --> 00:41:27.693
I said, "You know very well

00:41:27.818 --> 00:41:29.111
that I am Dr. Nash

00:41:29.195 --> 00:41:32.156
and I'm not examining
any goddamn grand baby."

00:41:32.239 --> 00:41:35.826
She got written up and she
came back later and said,

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:36.827
"I'm really sorry.

00:41:36.911 --> 00:41:39.288
I know very well that you are
Dr. Nash."

00:41:39.371 --> 00:41:41.123
I said, "Just you don't forget
it now.

00:41:41.207 --> 00:41:43.542
The next time you see a
black doctor doing something,

00:41:43.667 --> 00:41:44.710
you won't do that."

00:41:44.835 --> 00:41:47.796
- Well, obviously we were
always brother and sister,

00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:51.800
but professionally we
would have been fighting.

00:41:51.884 --> 00:41:53.302
(laughter)

00:41:53.385 --> 00:41:55.846
- Dr. Howard Venable is
another great example.

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.182
He was a very well recognized

00:41:59.225 --> 00:42:00.851
African-American
ophthalmologist.

00:42:00.935 --> 00:42:03.020
Many of these people really
became leaders

00:42:03.103 --> 00:42:04.271
in their medical fields,

00:42:04.396 --> 00:42:07.691
not just among black physicians,
but among all physicians,

00:42:07.775 --> 00:42:09.235
right? So,

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:11.153
they would have sort of,

00:42:11.237 --> 00:42:14.156
have positions in the
National Medical Association,

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:17.409
the black analog of the
American Medical Association.

00:42:17.535 --> 00:42:18.702
To have high ranking positions

00:42:18.786 --> 00:42:21.497
in the American Medical
Association,

00:42:21.580 --> 00:42:25.084
to be widely regarded in
their specialty fields

00:42:25.209 --> 00:42:26.585
is quite impressive.

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:31.215
I don't think you will find
that sort of, you know,

00:42:31.298 --> 00:42:34.718
sort of storied cohort of alumni

00:42:35.761 --> 00:42:37.930
from any other black hospital,
really.

00:42:38.055 --> 00:42:39.723
- I look at the Homer G.
Phillips story

00:42:39.807 --> 00:42:43.352
and it reminds me of the
Tuskegee Airmen.

00:42:43.435 --> 00:42:44.853
Think about that, and are you,

00:42:44.937 --> 00:42:47.106
you're telling someone
that they can't do it,

00:42:47.231 --> 00:42:51.402
they can't be a fighter pilot,
that they can't compete?

00:42:51.485 --> 00:42:54.321
Well, the woman and the men of
color

00:42:55.781 --> 00:42:57.908
who walked through Homer
G. Phillips Hospital

00:42:57.992 --> 00:43:00.369
were my Tuskegee Airmen.

00:43:00.452 --> 00:43:03.289
They said, "Okay, world,
take a look at this, okay?

00:43:03.414 --> 00:43:05.708
"Take a snapshot 'cause you're
not gonna,

00:43:05.791 --> 00:43:07.376
this is gonna be something you,

00:43:07.459 --> 00:43:09.545
we're gonna blow your socks
off.

00:43:09.628 --> 00:43:11.880
We're gonna show you
how it should be done."

00:43:11.964 --> 00:43:16.218
("This is How We Do It" by
Montell Jordan)

00:43:16.302 --> 00:43:20.889
♪ This is how we do it

00:43:20.973 --> 00:43:25.561
♪ This is how we do it

00:43:25.644 --> 00:43:30.149
♪ This is how we do it

00:43:30.274 --> 00:43:33.235
♪ This is how we do it

00:43:33.319 --> 00:43:35.738
- Well Homer G. Phillips,

00:43:35.821 --> 00:43:40.242
because it was a magnet for
talent,

00:43:42.828 --> 00:43:47.166
people took great pride and
they wanted to be the best

00:43:49.001 --> 00:43:51.629
and they were made to
feel proud by being here

00:43:51.712 --> 00:43:53.756
and they still kept in touch
with these

00:43:53.839 --> 00:43:54.757
long after they left.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:57.301
Many of them stayed in St.
Louis,

00:43:57.384 --> 00:44:00.679
many of the doctors I grew up
knowing

00:44:00.804 --> 00:44:03.641
I became friends with them in
later life.

00:44:03.724 --> 00:44:05.142
They stayed here.

00:44:05.225 --> 00:44:07.478
- My history with Homer G.
Phillips

00:44:07.561 --> 00:44:09.271
goes all the way back to infancy

00:44:09.355 --> 00:44:12.149
because I grew up only a block
and a half

00:44:12.232 --> 00:44:13.776
away from the hospital

00:44:13.859 --> 00:44:16.779
and it was the main hospital in
the city.

00:44:16.862 --> 00:44:18.989
In 1958 when I graduated,

00:44:19.073 --> 00:44:22.493
the white hospitals had opened
up

00:44:22.576 --> 00:44:23.869
and I applied for an internship

00:44:23.994 --> 00:44:27.456
at Pontiac General Hospital
in Pontiac, Michigan.

00:44:27.539 --> 00:44:30.542
Went there for a year and I
was gonna take a residency

00:44:30.668 --> 00:44:32.628
in pathology and I got to
thinking

00:44:32.711 --> 00:44:36.298
that I wanted to do
something to help people.

00:44:36.382 --> 00:44:39.885
I didn't wanna have
dead people as patients

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:43.055
and I thought maybe,
there was no technology,

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:45.557
and I thought maybe I
needed to get into a field

00:44:45.683 --> 00:44:47.309
where I could do something.

00:44:47.393 --> 00:44:49.687
I mean, just not standing
around and watch people die

00:44:49.770 --> 00:44:52.481
because we didn't know
what the diagnosis was.

00:44:52.564 --> 00:44:55.317
I called my mother and asked her

00:44:55.401 --> 00:44:58.320
if she would call her
brother, Dr. Gaitkins,

00:44:58.404 --> 00:45:01.907
and if he would call Dr.
Sinkler, Dr. William Sinkler,

00:45:02.032 --> 00:45:04.702
who was the head of
surgery at Homer Phillips

00:45:04.785 --> 00:45:07.663
and Dr. Sinkler told my mother,

00:45:07.746 --> 00:45:10.499
"Tell him to be here
July 1st at nine o'clock

00:45:10.582 --> 00:45:12.084
in uniform ready to go to
work."

00:45:12.209 --> 00:45:14.670
- You felt immediately
that you could relate

00:45:14.753 --> 00:45:16.839
to the individual that was there

00:45:16.922 --> 00:45:19.550
regardless of their stature

00:45:19.633 --> 00:45:22.970
and the hospital was a staffed
completely,

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:25.848
that is in the medical aspect

00:45:25.931 --> 00:45:29.768
and in the business aspect, by
blacks.

00:45:29.893 --> 00:45:32.604
So you did, you felt quite at
home.

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:37.192
I think that feeling like
that is conducive to learning.

00:45:38.819 --> 00:45:41.864
- The internship at the time,
'cause this is over rote

00:45:41.947 --> 00:45:44.241
was called a rotating
internship,

00:45:44.324 --> 00:45:48.871
in which you had to span
like three months on surgery,

00:45:48.954 --> 00:45:52.374
three months on medicine,
two months on OB-GYN,

00:45:52.458 --> 00:45:55.919
and then you have like
maybe one month of elective

00:45:56.003 --> 00:45:59.131
like urology or ear, nose and
throat,

00:45:59.256 --> 00:46:01.592
and maybe pathology.

00:46:01.675 --> 00:46:03.552
So on my three months of
surgery,

00:46:03.635 --> 00:46:05.137
I think I learned more surgery

00:46:05.262 --> 00:46:08.557
than most people learned
around the country.

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:11.560
- Dr. Sinkler was medical
director at that time

00:46:11.643 --> 00:46:13.896
and he had come to Howard
University

00:46:13.979 --> 00:46:16.607
and met with students
who may be interested

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:18.817
and he told us if we wanted to
come there,

00:46:18.942 --> 00:46:22.446
he would guarantee us that
we would have a position.

00:46:22.529 --> 00:46:25.073
That's why I came to Homer
Phillips,

00:46:25.157 --> 00:46:27.576
although I had known about it,

00:46:27.659 --> 00:46:31.914
but there were no women
doctors of any race in my town.

00:46:31.997 --> 00:46:33.248
So I didn't think that way.

00:46:33.332 --> 00:46:35.834
I went to Howard to
major in home economics,

00:46:35.959 --> 00:46:37.920
foods and nutrition, not
medicine,

00:46:38.003 --> 00:46:40.255
but I think I was leaning that
way.

00:46:40.339 --> 00:46:42.633
- So I came to Homer Phillips
Hospital

00:46:42.716 --> 00:46:46.136
and the dental program was a
relatively minor program there,

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:50.098
but my own education there
was assisted very much

00:46:50.182 --> 00:46:52.976
by the young physicians in
training.

00:46:54.520 --> 00:46:56.480
So when I came there,
I was in my twenties,

00:46:56.563 --> 00:46:59.274
as were most of the other young
people

00:46:59.358 --> 00:47:01.652
but St. Louis at that time was

00:47:03.362 --> 00:47:06.156
a much more vibrant city than it
is today

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.617
and of course the time I spent
in Homer Phillips Hospital

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:12.454
meant that I knew people about
my age

00:47:12.538 --> 00:47:14.122
from around the country.

00:47:14.206 --> 00:47:15.541
From as far west as California,

00:47:15.666 --> 00:47:18.627
south as Louisiana, North
Carolina.

00:47:18.710 --> 00:47:22.005
They all came here to
train surgical specialties.

00:47:22.089 --> 00:47:24.508
I spent two years in St. Louis,

00:47:26.510 --> 00:47:28.637
and then I went to the military.

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:30.055
I had an obligation.

00:47:30.180 --> 00:47:32.474
(tense music)

00:47:34.017 --> 00:47:36.478
- I served in the Navy in World
War II

00:47:36.562 --> 00:47:42.651
and I went in I think it was
1944

00:47:43.944 --> 00:47:45.487
at the height of the war,

00:47:45.571 --> 00:47:48.490
war was really going on
strongly at that time

00:47:48.574 --> 00:47:50.409
and they were drafting everyone

00:47:50.534 --> 00:47:52.995
that would turn 18 years of age.

00:47:54.246 --> 00:47:56.999
So I turned 18 and did
not get to go to my prom

00:47:57.082 --> 00:47:59.668
because I had received
a letter in the mailbox

00:47:59.751 --> 00:48:01.753
saying greetings, and you're to
come

00:48:01.879 --> 00:48:04.506
and have an examination.

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:06.842
Went up to Chicago, Illinois,

00:48:06.925 --> 00:48:09.845
and had the examination
and when I got back,

00:48:09.928 --> 00:48:12.264
I had my induction notice in the
mailbox,

00:48:12.389 --> 00:48:13.390
it was that quick.

00:48:13.473 --> 00:48:16.310
- After my internship from
1963-'64,

00:48:18.437 --> 00:48:21.899
I started my residency
July the 1st

00:48:23.233 --> 00:48:25.360
of 1964,

00:48:26.486 --> 00:48:27.696
gonna make sure I get these
dates correct.

00:48:27.779 --> 00:48:29.031
Then I was interrupted.

00:48:29.114 --> 00:48:30.741
I was drafted in 1966.

00:48:31.950 --> 00:48:35.370
I came back in '68 and finished
in '69.

00:48:35.454 --> 00:48:36.788
- January of 1967.

00:48:38.248 --> 00:48:40.542
It was cold, it was about 10
below zero

00:48:40.626 --> 00:48:43.295
and my wife called me and said,

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:45.255
"You got a strange letter here,

00:48:45.339 --> 00:48:46.381
I don't know what it means."

00:48:46.465 --> 00:48:49.092
I said, "Well, open it up," and
she says,

00:48:49.176 --> 00:48:51.219
"It's from the United States
government,

00:48:51.303 --> 00:48:52.930
and it says greetings from the
President

00:48:53.013 --> 00:48:54.556
of the United States."

00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:56.808
That was how they drafted
your back in the days

00:48:56.934 --> 00:48:58.226
and so I got drafted.

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:01.146
Mario was my son, he was

00:49:02.272 --> 00:49:03.649
almost four

00:49:04.399 --> 00:49:06.818
and we adjusted to it.

00:49:07.653 --> 00:49:08.737
I just, I went.

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:12.240
- I didn't want to choose
medicine.

00:49:12.324 --> 00:49:16.495
I preferred chemistry, I
wanted to be a chemist,

00:49:16.620 --> 00:49:20.791
but they were not hiring
any black chemists.

00:49:20.874 --> 00:49:25.087
I had the top honors in
chemistry at Howard university.

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:29.299
So I noticed that the fellows
who went to medical school

00:49:29.383 --> 00:49:31.218
were not going to Korea.

00:49:32.886 --> 00:49:35.847
So I said, well, I guess I
better go down there then,

00:49:35.973 --> 00:49:40.102
I don't wanna go in military as
a private.

00:49:40.185 --> 00:49:43.438
Yeah, I was chief of
surgery in Ozen, Korea,

00:49:43.522 --> 00:49:46.942
one of the most significant
things that I accomplished

00:49:47.025 --> 00:49:50.028
was integrating the air force
hospital.

00:49:50.153 --> 00:49:53.115
The Koreans who would get hit
around

00:49:53.198 --> 00:49:56.785
and they wouldn't let them,
we could not admit them,

00:49:56.868 --> 00:50:00.163
but I did admit one and
the colonel came in,

00:50:00.247 --> 00:50:02.332
said I couldn't do that.

00:50:02.416 --> 00:50:06.211
And so I told him what
reasons I had.

00:50:06.336 --> 00:50:07.796
No point in sending him out to
die

00:50:07.879 --> 00:50:10.132
when we could take care
of him and he told me,

00:50:10.215 --> 00:50:13.135
"Sit down." He said
"Well, Jackson,

00:50:13.218 --> 00:50:17.055
you guys vote tonight and if
y'all decide

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:18.724
to treat this here Korean,

00:50:18.849 --> 00:50:20.892
I'm not gonna stand in your
way."

00:50:21.018 --> 00:50:22.894
So that night we were
all draftees, you know,

00:50:23.020 --> 00:50:24.980
basically we've already admit
the Korean.

00:50:25.063 --> 00:50:29.276
- So my dad was in medical
school

00:50:30.235 --> 00:50:34.031
during World War II

00:50:35.032 --> 00:50:37.576
during the draft for World War
II,

00:50:37.701 --> 00:50:41.413
and during that time they
allowed him

00:50:41.538 --> 00:50:45.751
to join the service and
continue with medical school.

00:50:46.918 --> 00:50:49.004
So he spent his time in medical
school

00:50:49.087 --> 00:50:50.672
in his last year of medical
school

00:50:50.756 --> 00:50:53.008
as part of being in the army.

00:50:53.091 --> 00:50:56.094
After that, he completed
his medical training,

00:50:56.219 --> 00:50:59.514
completed his residency,

00:51:00.432 --> 00:51:03.185
spent a year in Chicago.

00:51:03.268 --> 00:51:05.020
Then during the Korean War

00:51:05.103 --> 00:51:08.231
that he was called back
into military service

00:51:08.315 --> 00:51:11.860
and that was quite a
disruptive event for him.

00:51:11.943 --> 00:51:15.447
Fortunately for him, they sent
him to a pediatric hospital

00:51:15.572 --> 00:51:18.867
in Wurtzburg where he met our
mother

00:51:19.993 --> 00:51:22.287
who was working as a
nurse there in the wards

00:51:22.412 --> 00:51:25.707
and they were married in
Germany.

00:51:25.791 --> 00:51:29.711
And then came back and
took up his practice

00:51:29.795 --> 00:51:31.379
and they worked together to
reestablish

00:51:31.463 --> 00:51:33.131
his practice in Los Angeles.

00:51:33.256 --> 00:51:35.550
- Well, well during my residency

00:51:35.634 --> 00:51:37.719
where I became friends with
quite a few,

00:51:37.803 --> 00:51:40.597
but I remember some more
than others of course,

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:44.017
you know, Robinson, Oliver Page,
McDonald,

00:51:45.977 --> 00:51:47.395
we were all residents.

00:51:47.479 --> 00:51:49.773
Some of us who are in different
fields.

00:51:49.856 --> 00:51:52.067
- Yeah, we met when we were
young kids.

00:51:52.150 --> 00:51:53.735
- At the YMCA camp.

00:51:53.819 --> 00:51:55.112
- The Camp River Cliff.

00:51:55.195 --> 00:51:57.948
So it turned out that I loved
camp.

00:51:58.031 --> 00:52:00.408
He couldn't stand it cause
he always wanted to eat

00:52:00.492 --> 00:52:02.786
and he went home to his mom
after 10 days.

00:52:02.869 --> 00:52:04.621
- No, they wouldn't come and get
me now.

00:52:04.704 --> 00:52:05.455
- They would come.

00:52:05.539 --> 00:52:06.832
- I called every day.

00:52:06.957 --> 00:52:08.625
They let you make one phone call
a day

00:52:08.708 --> 00:52:11.169
and I called home to ask
of them to come and get me

00:52:11.294 --> 00:52:12.420
and they wouldn't do it.

00:52:12.504 --> 00:52:14.089
- So this is how we first hooked
up

00:52:14.172 --> 00:52:17.259
and then years passed and
we didn't see each other

00:52:17.342 --> 00:52:18.844
and then we came back as friends

00:52:18.969 --> 00:52:20.428
later on at Homer G. Phillips.

00:52:20.512 --> 00:52:24.141
- Now I remember Dr.
Robinson and Dr. MacDonald

00:52:24.224 --> 00:52:25.767
because as I had said before,

00:52:25.851 --> 00:52:27.936
we did a rotating internship.

00:52:28.019 --> 00:52:32.440
So as spent I guess
four months on surgery,

00:52:33.316 --> 00:52:34.776
two months on female surgery,

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:38.321
two months on male surgery
and we were of course interns

00:52:38.405 --> 00:52:39.531
and they were the residents.

00:52:39.656 --> 00:52:43.118
So we were allowed the first
line to draw the blood,

00:52:43.201 --> 00:52:45.036
take the history, write up
everything,

00:52:45.162 --> 00:52:47.622
as they used to call it the
quote scutwork

00:52:47.706 --> 00:52:50.542
and get the patients
ready for the residents.

00:52:50.667 --> 00:52:52.544
And I can remember,
Dr. McDonald and I,

00:52:52.669 --> 00:52:55.672
at three o'clock in the
morning on a Saturday night

00:52:55.755 --> 00:52:56.798
or early Sunday morning.

00:52:56.882 --> 00:52:59.634
We had tried to put a
300 pound man on slab

00:52:59.718 --> 00:53:01.136
so we could do an arteriogram

00:53:01.219 --> 00:53:02.888
and he was just about as small
as I,

00:53:03.013 --> 00:53:05.056
but I think we finally got it
done.

00:53:05.182 --> 00:53:07.225
It took about three
hours 'cause the patient

00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:09.686
would not help us because
he was shot by the police

00:53:09.769 --> 00:53:11.062
so he wasn't doing a thing.

00:53:11.188 --> 00:53:13.481
- We had a poker game that we
would play

00:53:13.565 --> 00:53:14.858
on the fifth floor,

00:53:14.941 --> 00:53:18.570
and we'd get paid I think
the first of the month,

00:53:18.695 --> 00:53:20.864
and about three days after that,

00:53:20.947 --> 00:53:22.365
all the money was gone.

00:53:22.449 --> 00:53:25.035
So we couldn't put any money in
the pot

00:53:25.118 --> 00:53:28.538
and we would play what
we called on the finger.

00:53:28.622 --> 00:53:31.333
- The public game ran on
Fridays,

00:53:31.416 --> 00:53:32.834
on payday till the money ran
out.

00:53:32.918 --> 00:53:35.003
It may run for three or four
hours

00:53:35.086 --> 00:53:36.671
or it may run all the way to
Saturday

00:53:36.755 --> 00:53:38.715
and then we started playing for
credit.

00:53:38.798 --> 00:53:40.675
We called it betting on the
finger

00:53:40.759 --> 00:53:43.511
where, "I owe this money,

00:53:43.595 --> 00:53:46.890
I bet you $50, I raise
your $100 on the finger,

00:53:46.973 --> 00:53:50.685
I ain't got $100, but it
will be in the pot later on."

00:53:50.769 --> 00:53:53.438
- We would keep record
of the fingers in the pot

00:53:53.563 --> 00:53:56.524
and then we'd have to settle
up when the next pay day came.

00:53:56.608 --> 00:53:58.526
- There was still
several bars around there

00:53:58.610 --> 00:53:59.778
that we would frequent.

00:53:59.903 --> 00:54:03.114
I know I remember one
called the Nutty Pine

00:54:03.240 --> 00:54:05.533
you know, we'd go there
early in the morning,

00:54:05.617 --> 00:54:08.703
sometimes we finished a round.

00:54:08.787 --> 00:54:11.373
- Course, as soon as we
left work, we went to a bar.

00:54:11.456 --> 00:54:12.624
We had our favorite hang outs,

00:54:12.749 --> 00:54:15.043
Sorento, the Pine Nut,

00:54:15.126 --> 00:54:18.088
what was that little place we'd
hang out?

00:54:18.171 --> 00:54:21.216
Ruby's Zodiac Lounge
over in East St. Louis.

00:54:21.299 --> 00:54:22.384
All the clubs, the Files Club,

00:54:22.467 --> 00:54:23.969
the Manhattan.
- I remember Ruby.

00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:27.472
- Yes, I do know Dr. Robinson
Jr.

00:54:27.597 --> 00:54:29.766
He is an obstetrician
gynecologist too.

00:54:29.849 --> 00:54:31.726
He was well ahead of me.

00:54:31.810 --> 00:54:36.648
I think he was probably six
to eight years ahead of me,

00:54:36.773 --> 00:54:40.485
but I knew him and the reason I
knew him,

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:43.571
I came here originally
as a research assistant

00:54:43.655 --> 00:54:45.573
at Washington University,

00:54:45.657 --> 00:54:48.910
but I lived in the dormitory
over here

00:54:48.994 --> 00:54:53.081
and the people in OB-GYN found
out that I was interested

00:54:53.164 --> 00:54:55.250
and so they would invite me down

00:54:55.333 --> 00:54:57.585
and I would do delivery on the,
you know,

00:54:57.669 --> 00:55:01.506
the watchful eyes and that's how
I met him

00:55:01.631 --> 00:55:04.134
and the residents on his level.

00:55:04.217 --> 00:55:07.262
- The difference in
working at a white hospital

00:55:07.345 --> 00:55:10.265
and a black hospital like
Homer G. Phillips Hospital

00:55:10.348 --> 00:55:13.018
was like dying and going to
heaven.

00:55:13.143 --> 00:55:17.605
Just that was the
difference and it was a lot.

00:55:17.689 --> 00:55:18.773
It was hard work though.

00:55:18.857 --> 00:55:21.026
I mean we worked extremely hard,

00:55:21.151 --> 00:55:22.152
but that was a good thing

00:55:22.235 --> 00:55:24.279
because we developed a good work
ethic.

00:55:24.362 --> 00:55:28.325
- Washington University was
the university affiliate

00:55:28.408 --> 00:55:32.037
that gave us accreditation
to have a residency.

00:55:32.162 --> 00:55:35.040
So all departments had
a great white father.

00:55:35.165 --> 00:55:37.125
They were like, they knew
everything.

00:55:37.208 --> 00:55:41.963
One time Dr. Monad, who
was our great white father,

00:55:42.047 --> 00:55:43.798
had examined his patient and
said

00:55:43.882 --> 00:55:46.634
the patient had an ovarian cyst.

00:55:46.718 --> 00:55:49.220
We said, no, the patient's got
ascites,

00:55:49.346 --> 00:55:51.806
which means a whole bunch
of fluid in the abdomen.

00:55:51.890 --> 00:55:53.641
So Dr. Smiley said,

00:55:53.725 --> 00:55:56.144
"Well, we need to wait and just
drain it."

00:55:56.227 --> 00:55:57.479
So Dr. Monad said,

00:55:57.562 --> 00:55:59.564
"We've got to take the
patient to surgery,"

00:55:59.689 --> 00:56:00.857
because the abdomen and it's
like,

00:56:00.940 --> 00:56:02.859
looks like she's nine months
pregnant.

00:56:02.942 --> 00:56:04.861
Dr. Monad is going to do
the operation, you know,

00:56:04.944 --> 00:56:07.364
because he gonna prove
to us colored people

00:56:07.447 --> 00:56:09.032
that we didn't know what we
doing.

00:56:09.115 --> 00:56:11.618
So knife cuts down, open her up,

00:56:12.952 --> 00:56:15.538
when he tapped the perineum,

00:56:15.622 --> 00:56:18.208
fluid squirted up to the light.

00:56:18.291 --> 00:56:21.002
It was about 6,000 cc's.

00:56:21.086 --> 00:56:24.172
That's like four gallons of
fluids.

00:56:24.255 --> 00:56:27.926
So he was chagrin
because Smiley was right.

00:56:28.051 --> 00:56:31.262
We're back in the back and
we're just cracking up.

00:56:31.388 --> 00:56:32.347
Well see, those are the kind of
things,

00:56:32.430 --> 00:56:34.099
the games we played, you know?

00:56:34.224 --> 00:56:35.725
- During the discussions,

00:56:35.809 --> 00:56:38.019
particularly around the nurses,
I mean,

00:56:38.103 --> 00:56:40.355
physicians are fine, but when
we talk about the nurses,

00:56:40.438 --> 00:56:41.940
what's really remarkable is the
fact

00:56:42.065 --> 00:56:43.400
that they were driving a lot

00:56:43.483 --> 00:56:45.860
of what was going on in the
hospital.

00:56:45.944 --> 00:56:47.862
Now they don't wanna say that
openly,

00:56:47.946 --> 00:56:51.574
but they really were just
remarkable driving force.

00:56:51.658 --> 00:56:53.743
I heard all the backstories

00:56:53.827 --> 00:56:55.203
about what goes on in the OR

00:56:55.286 --> 00:56:57.205
and who really was moving things
along

00:56:57.288 --> 00:57:02.043
and it's just almost like
the hidden figures story,

00:57:02.127 --> 00:57:03.878
that really appealed to me

00:57:03.962 --> 00:57:06.423
just hearing about those figures

00:57:07.632 --> 00:57:10.051
that drove the excellence of the
hospital.

00:57:10.135 --> 00:57:12.720
- I also think one thing
that's very important

00:57:12.804 --> 00:57:15.223
about the hospital is that it
acted

00:57:15.306 --> 00:57:17.809
as an engine of change

00:57:17.934 --> 00:57:20.895
for many of these nurses and
doctors

00:57:20.979 --> 00:57:25.650
that many people had, had come
from very poor backgrounds.

00:57:25.775 --> 00:57:27.652
There were people who
came from the rural South

00:57:27.777 --> 00:57:29.612
who'd never left their
communities,

00:57:29.696 --> 00:57:33.658
but they somehow found the
means to leave their homes,

00:57:33.783 --> 00:57:35.452
take a train or a bus,

00:57:35.535 --> 00:57:38.621
take this journey to St.
Louis and start a new life

00:57:38.705 --> 00:57:41.416
and by the time they
finished the nursing program

00:57:41.499 --> 00:57:45.628
or their residency, they were
part of the middle class.

00:57:45.712 --> 00:57:48.089
- The way I learned about Homer
Phillips

00:57:48.173 --> 00:57:49.507
and the school of nursing,

00:57:49.632 --> 00:57:52.469
while I was a junior in high
school,

00:57:52.552 --> 00:57:57.432
I saw a brochure with
information about Homer Phillips

00:57:57.515 --> 00:57:59.767
and it was in color,

00:57:59.851 --> 00:58:04.147
and the young ladies were
in those beautiful uniforms.

00:58:05.565 --> 00:58:09.194
It was the most beautiful thing
had seen

00:58:09.319 --> 00:58:13.615
and I had always enjoyed
taking care of things.

00:58:13.698 --> 00:58:15.617
So I said, that's for me.

00:58:17.035 --> 00:58:20.497
And Ms. Minnie Eagle was the
director of nursing then,

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:22.123
so she accepted me.

00:58:22.207 --> 00:58:26.628
So September of 1952

00:58:27.504 --> 00:58:28.796
I ended up here in St. Louis,

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:31.799
at Homer G. Phillips
Hospital School of Nursing.

00:58:31.883 --> 00:58:34.135
- I was born in Detroit,
Michigan.

00:58:34.219 --> 00:58:36.846
How I became acquainted
with Homer G. Phillips

00:58:36.930 --> 00:58:40.350
was that the nurses
came to my mother's home

00:58:40.433 --> 00:58:42.477
when my sisters were born,

00:58:42.560 --> 00:58:46.856
and so I watched their
performance in nursing care.

00:58:46.940 --> 00:58:48.858
And I agreed with all of those
nurses.

00:58:48.942 --> 00:58:51.819
- My sister used to
pick, was able to pick up

00:58:51.903 --> 00:58:54.989
a PMOX in Monroe late at night

00:58:55.073 --> 00:58:55.907
and she would hear about

00:58:56.032 --> 00:58:57.825
the legacy of Homer Phillips
Hospital

00:58:57.909 --> 00:59:00.203
and I always wanted to be a
nurse

00:59:00.286 --> 00:59:03.706
and there was a Homer Phillips
graduate

00:59:03.790 --> 00:59:06.501
and a Homer Phillips doctor
who had trained here,

00:59:06.584 --> 00:59:09.254
a couple of them lived in my
city.

00:59:09.379 --> 00:59:13.716
And Harriet Foster, who was
a Homer Phillips graduate,

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:15.176
encouraged my parents

00:59:15.260 --> 00:59:18.513
to allow me to come to Homer
Phillips

00:59:18.596 --> 00:59:21.849
and we were even featured in a
book

00:59:21.933 --> 00:59:23.601
called The Warmth of Other Suns.

00:59:23.726 --> 00:59:25.853
- I am from Jackson, Tennessee

00:59:25.937 --> 00:59:29.440
and I have family members
who were employed here

00:59:29.566 --> 00:59:32.110
and we did not have a hospital

00:59:32.235 --> 00:59:35.113
for black people in Jackson.

00:59:35.238 --> 00:59:37.699
And I came to St. Louis as a
child

00:59:37.782 --> 00:59:41.202
and had my tonsils removed
at Homer Phillips hospital.

00:59:41.286 --> 00:59:43.746
- I'm from Vicksburg,
Mississippi.

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:47.917
When I graduated from high
school in 1963,

00:59:48.001 --> 00:59:51.546
Mercy Hospital there
head a nursing school,

00:59:51.629 --> 00:59:54.632
but they did not accept blacks

00:59:54.757 --> 00:59:57.885
and I was scheduled to
attend St. Mary's College

00:59:57.969 --> 01:00:01.097
in St. Mary's, Kansas, and all
set to go

01:00:01.180 --> 01:00:02.890
and one day in the early spring,

01:00:02.974 --> 01:00:04.892
I walked through the principal's
office

01:00:04.976 --> 01:00:08.062
and I saw that brochure
from Homer Phillips

01:00:08.146 --> 01:00:10.565
and I'd said, well, you
know, may I have this?

01:00:10.648 --> 01:00:14.235
And of course she was, it
was okay with her and I,

01:00:14.319 --> 01:00:16.904
you know, read the whole
book and that was it.

01:00:16.988 --> 01:00:20.450
- I lived about five miles
north from Homer G. Phillips.

01:00:20.533 --> 01:00:23.536
I always felt I wanted to help
people,

01:00:24.829 --> 01:00:26.414
but my parents really helped me

01:00:26.497 --> 01:00:29.417
and inspired me to realize that
dream.

01:00:29.500 --> 01:00:32.086
So not only was I born here,

01:00:32.170 --> 01:00:34.422
but several of my other family
members

01:00:34.505 --> 01:00:38.301
and then my daughter and my
mother as well worked here

01:00:38.384 --> 01:00:43.139
as an OB over on One North,
which was the medical floor.

01:00:43.222 --> 01:00:45.767
- I was born here in St. Louis

01:00:45.850 --> 01:00:48.645
and my aunt, name is Estelle
Massey,

01:00:51.230 --> 01:00:54.651
was a nurse here and I
wanted to be like her

01:00:54.734 --> 01:00:57.445
and my dad would've talked me
into it,

01:00:57.528 --> 01:01:01.491
he wanted me to be a nurse
I think because she was one.

01:01:01.574 --> 01:01:03.618
- I'm from Evansville, Indiana,

01:01:03.701 --> 01:01:07.163
and back in the '50s,
there were only two schools

01:01:07.246 --> 01:01:09.999
accepting African American
students

01:01:10.083 --> 01:01:11.793
and Homer G. Phillips was one

01:01:11.876 --> 01:01:15.463
and that's how I ended
up in Homer G. Phillips.

01:01:15.546 --> 01:01:19.676
Plus I was in the Future
Nurses Club in my high school

01:01:19.759 --> 01:01:21.844
and the nurse's name was Jackson

01:01:21.928 --> 01:01:23.388
and my maiden name is Jackson

01:01:23.513 --> 01:01:25.306
and I wanted to be just like her

01:01:25.390 --> 01:01:28.976
so that's how I ended up being a
nurse.

01:01:29.060 --> 01:01:31.354
- I lived four or five blocks

01:01:31.437 --> 01:01:33.648
from Homer Phillips as a child.

01:01:33.731 --> 01:01:36.442
I recall many nights as a young
kid

01:01:37.568 --> 01:01:39.904
and taken to Homer G. Phillips
Hospital

01:01:40.029 --> 01:01:41.656
for one element or another.

01:01:41.739 --> 01:01:44.325
I always, even as a young child,

01:01:44.409 --> 01:01:47.829
remember wanting to take
care and heal people.

01:01:47.912 --> 01:01:50.665
So nursing has always been in my
blood,

01:01:50.748 --> 01:01:54.419
and Homer G. Phillips was
right in my neighborhood.

01:01:54.544 --> 01:01:57.088
I was so very proud to see the
students

01:01:57.213 --> 01:02:00.883
with their uniforms and their
white caps.

01:02:00.967 --> 01:02:03.344
- I was born here in St. Louis
and I lived in The Ville,

01:02:03.428 --> 01:02:05.012
so we were in walking distance.

01:02:05.096 --> 01:02:07.223
I had six family members

01:02:07.306 --> 01:02:09.100
that worked at Homer G.
Phillips.

01:02:09.225 --> 01:02:12.937
So family was, the family
was full of medicine,

01:02:14.147 --> 01:02:17.525
that was always my
desire to be in medicine.

01:02:17.608 --> 01:02:20.236
- I was born in Memphis

01:02:20.319 --> 01:02:22.780
and there was the nursing school
there

01:02:22.905 --> 01:02:25.032
and in a crunch I got there,

01:02:26.576 --> 01:02:29.203
they only took Caucasians

01:02:29.287 --> 01:02:32.039
and so I was not accepted.

01:02:32.123 --> 01:02:35.460
A lady in a church told us about
St. Louis

01:02:36.794 --> 01:02:38.421
and Homer G. Phillips.

01:02:38.504 --> 01:02:41.883
So all five kids got in the back
seat.

01:02:41.966 --> 01:02:45.928
I was accepted and my
parents drove us up here

01:02:46.012 --> 01:02:47.597
and put us in the hands

01:02:47.680 --> 01:02:50.475
of the people at Homer G.
Phillips

01:02:50.600 --> 01:02:52.560
and it was quite a reward.

01:02:54.312 --> 01:02:58.775
- I remember Homer Phillips
about the age of 10 or 11,

01:02:58.858 --> 01:03:02.612
because I had asthma and
I spent so many nights

01:03:03.988 --> 01:03:06.407
at this hospital and so many
days,

01:03:06.491 --> 01:03:10.244
weeks admitted to the pediatric
floor.

01:03:11.454 --> 01:03:14.624
They didn't think I would
graduate high school

01:03:14.707 --> 01:03:16.250
because I had to have teachers

01:03:16.334 --> 01:03:19.462
to come tutor me in the
hospital.

01:03:19.545 --> 01:03:23.466
Fortunately I did graduate
and I went to nursing school,

01:03:23.549 --> 01:03:26.844
graduated and came back to Homer
Phillips

01:03:26.969 --> 01:03:30.014
to work in that same emergency
room

01:03:30.139 --> 01:03:33.100
with the same nurses that took
care me.

01:03:33.184 --> 01:03:34.852
(laughter)

01:03:34.977 --> 01:03:37.772
And it was just an awesome
feeling.

01:03:37.855 --> 01:03:40.817
- When I arrived at Philips,

01:03:42.360 --> 01:03:45.988
I was met by an upperclassman

01:03:47.323 --> 01:03:49.784
and she was my mentor

01:03:49.867 --> 01:03:55.122
and she continued to be my
friend throughout nursing.

01:03:55.998 --> 01:03:57.708
She was just like an advisor

01:03:57.834 --> 01:04:01.796
to sort of keep a country
girl straight.

01:04:02.922 --> 01:04:05.675
You know, to learn the
rules, regulations.

01:04:08.094 --> 01:04:10.137
Show me something about the
city.

01:04:10.221 --> 01:04:12.139
Show me around the hospital

01:04:12.223 --> 01:04:15.810
because it was a big place for
me.

01:04:18.563 --> 01:04:21.065
Rules and regulations, you know,

01:04:21.190 --> 01:04:22.900
things to do to have fun

01:04:23.025 --> 01:04:25.653
and things to do to stay out of
trouble.

01:04:25.736 --> 01:04:28.573
And a few things to get in
trouble.

01:04:28.698 --> 01:04:30.157
- [Jobyna] Our instructors were
excellent.

01:04:30.241 --> 01:04:31.701
- [Woman In Gray] They
were, they were excellent.

01:04:31.784 --> 01:04:35.997
- They would not accept
anything but excellence from us.

01:04:37.290 --> 01:04:41.002
You had to excel to be a
Homer G. Phillips nurse.

01:04:41.085 --> 01:04:45.214
You could not just walk out
in your uniform and cap.

01:04:45.298 --> 01:04:47.174
You can not go down to Billy
Barks

01:04:47.258 --> 01:04:49.677
as you heard them talk about
before

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:51.387
in your uniform and cap.

01:04:51.470 --> 01:04:54.056
You could not go down to the
Blue Rooms

01:04:54.140 --> 01:04:56.684
in your uniform and cap.

01:04:56.767 --> 01:05:01.188
So we were taught those
high standards of nursing

01:05:01.272 --> 01:05:03.608
and how to be a nurse in a
community

01:05:03.733 --> 01:05:06.861
and take care of patients
and be well respected.

01:05:06.944 --> 01:05:09.864
- One of the beautiful
things about the training

01:05:09.947 --> 01:05:13.618
that we received as
nurses at Homer Phillips,

01:05:13.743 --> 01:05:17.705
well, we were taught
to be all round person.

01:05:17.788 --> 01:05:21.375
We would, we went to the
ballgames.

01:05:21.459 --> 01:05:25.796
We went to the opera,
we went to the symphony,

01:05:25.922 --> 01:05:29.383
we went to churches of
various denominations

01:05:31.636 --> 01:05:35.640
because the feeling was
that to be a good nurse,

01:05:35.765 --> 01:05:39.644
you need to be a rounded person

01:05:40.645 --> 01:05:43.314
and we were exposed to all of
that

01:05:43.439 --> 01:05:45.608
and I really appreciate it.

01:05:45.691 --> 01:05:47.985
It has made me a better person.

01:05:48.110 --> 01:05:51.447
- Our director of nurses
made an appointment

01:05:51.530 --> 01:05:55.117
to go to Famous-Barr
that was a costume room,

01:05:56.285 --> 01:05:57.912
The couture room.

01:05:57.995 --> 01:05:59.413
It's one of these, like,

01:05:59.497 --> 01:06:02.416
Bloomingdale and those kinds of
stores.

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:05.127
Well, Famous-Barr had a costume
room.

01:06:05.211 --> 01:06:10.424
We were taken to the costume
room to get our shoes.

01:06:11.342 --> 01:06:14.136
We would visit especially

01:06:14.845 --> 01:06:17.014
made by an individual.

01:06:18.057 --> 01:06:20.017
We were taken down there on the
bus

01:06:20.142 --> 01:06:21.811
and each one of us individuals

01:06:21.894 --> 01:06:23.688
would get measured for our
shoes.

01:06:23.813 --> 01:06:26.148
We wore the high heeled granny
shoes,

01:06:26.232 --> 01:06:28.818
long pointed toe if you've seen
it

01:06:28.901 --> 01:06:32.363
with silk shoe strings

01:06:32.488 --> 01:06:34.532
and they had to be clean

01:06:35.533 --> 01:06:38.452
and straightened in your shoes.

01:06:38.536 --> 01:06:40.454
They could not be twisted.

01:06:40.538 --> 01:06:41.789
Those high heel shoes

01:06:41.872 --> 01:06:45.001
were the most comfortable
shoes, that heel,

01:06:45.084 --> 01:06:47.837
and we called them mother
clumpers.

01:06:49.672 --> 01:06:51.674
We called them mother clumpers,

01:06:51.757 --> 01:06:54.468
but they saved our feet.

01:06:54.552 --> 01:06:56.887
You see, she wanted us to have
the best,

01:06:57.013 --> 01:06:59.223
we always had the best.

01:06:59.348 --> 01:07:01.684
Even when we had to fight for
it,

01:07:01.767 --> 01:07:04.020
they fought for us to get the
best

01:07:04.103 --> 01:07:06.689
or else we wouldn't
settle for anything less.

01:07:06.772 --> 01:07:09.859
- There was Dr. Smiley, our
OB-GYN man.

01:07:13.070 --> 01:07:16.574
The best OB-GYN doctor I've ever
seen.

01:07:18.242 --> 01:07:20.911
We used to laugh in the
operating room.

01:07:21.037 --> 01:07:23.330
He hummed all the time.

01:07:24.165 --> 01:07:26.584
- Smiley, who was an OB GYN,

01:07:27.668 --> 01:07:30.921
prided himself, as the doctors
all said,

01:07:31.047 --> 01:07:35.009
he never said it, even
though he taught us OB-GYN,

01:07:36.385 --> 01:07:38.846
that he could take out a
hysterectomy,

01:07:38.929 --> 01:07:41.015
he'd do a hysterectomy in 20
minutes

01:07:41.098 --> 01:07:44.018
and then when you talk to
those operating room nurses,

01:07:44.101 --> 01:07:46.228
he was whistling the whole time

01:07:46.312 --> 01:07:48.522
and doing this surgery and in 20
minutes,

01:07:48.606 --> 01:07:49.940
the uterus is out,

01:07:50.066 --> 01:07:53.194
tumor and everything else
going to the pathology lab.

01:07:53.277 --> 01:07:54.779
That was Dr. Smiley, he
used that kind of speed.

01:07:54.904 --> 01:07:57.198
- And he only used three
instruments,

01:07:57.281 --> 01:08:00.451
hemostat, scissors,

01:08:00.576 --> 01:08:01.702
Metzenbaum scissors

01:08:01.786 --> 01:08:03.913
and the needle holder.

01:08:03.996 --> 01:08:07.208
And he kept the scissors on his
finger

01:08:07.291 --> 01:08:08.709
and he would flip them around.

01:08:08.793 --> 01:08:11.128
He would suture, tie, cut,

01:08:11.253 --> 01:08:13.547
and use wet sponge so
you didn't have to worry

01:08:13.631 --> 01:08:16.884
about losing sponges in
patients at that time.

01:08:18.135 --> 01:08:21.430
He was really good, he was
really fast.

01:08:21.514 --> 01:08:25.017
- And he didn't ask you
for his instruments,

01:08:26.268 --> 01:08:29.480
you were supposed to know, and
we did.

01:08:29.605 --> 01:08:31.774
- The other thing we had to
learn

01:08:31.857 --> 01:08:35.569
the anticipation of what
the doctor's going to need.

01:08:35.653 --> 01:08:39.824
So therefore your instruments
were placed in that order.

01:08:39.949 --> 01:08:44.120
Know he's gonna need his scalpel
first to make the incision,

01:08:44.203 --> 01:08:46.997
you know secondly, he's
gonna need some clamps

01:08:47.123 --> 01:08:49.750
or forceps as we call 'em,

01:08:49.834 --> 01:08:51.919
to clamp off the blood vessels.

01:08:52.002 --> 01:08:55.464
You may need your retractors
to pull back the muscles.

01:08:55.548 --> 01:08:59.426
Then you're gonna need
certainly ready to suture up

01:08:59.510 --> 01:09:02.263
after removing with the scissors
and all,

01:09:02.346 --> 01:09:05.432
you're gonna need your
black silk or your cat gut,

01:09:05.516 --> 01:09:08.435
your scissors, your needle and
thread.

01:09:08.519 --> 01:09:11.522
- We were taught nursing was a
career.

01:09:14.650 --> 01:09:19.780
I believe now as observation,

01:09:20.990 --> 01:09:24.827
that nursing is a job and
it makes a difference.

01:09:24.910 --> 01:09:28.205
- Most members of St. Louis
community

01:09:28.330 --> 01:09:30.499
are aware of the school of
nursing here.

01:09:30.583 --> 01:09:33.627
They are aware that doctors
trained here

01:09:33.711 --> 01:09:36.046
as interns and residents.

01:09:36.172 --> 01:09:40.384
But there were also three
other schools for students.

01:09:40.509 --> 01:09:44.013
One was the school for
medical technologists,

01:09:44.096 --> 01:09:47.683
one was the school for x-ray
technicians

01:09:47.766 --> 01:09:52.479
and the third was the school
for medical record librarians.

01:09:52.563 --> 01:09:54.857
That is the school that I
attended,

01:09:54.940 --> 01:09:56.901
for medical record librarians.

01:09:57.026 --> 01:09:59.904
I was one of those African
American babies

01:10:00.029 --> 01:10:01.989
born in Homer Philips

01:10:02.072 --> 01:10:04.992
during the time of segregated
health care

01:10:05.075 --> 01:10:10.497
and I became aware of the
school and the profession

01:10:11.874 --> 01:10:15.836
through our family physician
who was Dr. Julius Sherard.

01:10:16.962 --> 01:10:19.757
He was also on the staff at
Homer Phillips

01:10:19.882 --> 01:10:23.594
and very proud and supportive of
his wife,

01:10:23.719 --> 01:10:26.513
Esther Sherard, who was the
founder

01:10:26.597 --> 01:10:28.682
of the School for Medical Record

01:10:28.766 --> 01:10:30.768
Librarians at Homer Phillips.

01:10:30.893 --> 01:10:35.606
Medical record librarians today,
that title does not exist.

01:10:37.066 --> 01:10:41.362
They're called health care
managers

01:10:41.445 --> 01:10:45.866
and it reflects the digital
age we currently live in.

01:10:45.950 --> 01:10:50.037
But when I was a student,
this is what we were called,

01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:53.958
Medical Record Librarians,
and this was our bible.

01:10:54.083 --> 01:10:55.626
This is the manual.

01:10:55.751 --> 01:10:57.878
Students throughout the United
States

01:10:57.962 --> 01:11:02.049
used this same textbook,
along with other textbooks,

01:11:02.132 --> 01:11:06.929
but this was what taught us
how our jobs should be done.

01:11:08.013 --> 01:11:10.557
- I think Homer G.
Phillips really achieved

01:11:10.641 --> 01:11:14.270
its highest point ironically, I
guess,

01:11:14.353 --> 01:11:17.606
just before desegregation
occurred

01:11:17.690 --> 01:11:22.486
because there was so many
fabulously talented residents

01:11:22.611 --> 01:11:24.238
who wanted to come here.

01:11:24.321 --> 01:11:27.825
As these people came to
have other opportunities

01:11:27.950 --> 01:11:29.451
in the United States,

01:11:29.535 --> 01:11:31.829
not as many super talented
people

01:11:31.954 --> 01:11:34.415
came to Homer G. Phillips
Hospital.

01:11:34.498 --> 01:11:36.417
- It started as early as 1945,

01:11:36.500 --> 01:11:38.585
with the Hill-Burton Act,

01:11:38.669 --> 01:11:40.587
really putting hospitals on
notice

01:11:40.671 --> 01:11:42.256
that they could not actively
discriminate,

01:11:42.339 --> 01:11:44.425
but yeah, by the 1960s,

01:11:45.259 --> 01:11:47.803
we really just start seeing

01:11:47.886 --> 01:11:51.181
the inability to recruit
the top notch surgeons

01:11:51.307 --> 01:11:53.767
of William Sinkler,

01:11:53.851 --> 01:11:56.812
and James Whittico, and Dr.
Robinson.

01:11:56.895 --> 01:11:59.189
- When we talked about
the closing of Homer G.,

01:11:59.315 --> 01:12:02.151
there was rumors for a number of
years

01:12:02.234 --> 01:12:03.110
'cause they said the city wanted

01:12:03.193 --> 01:12:05.112
to get out of the hospital
business.

01:12:07.031 --> 01:12:10.200
And so when it finally
came down,

01:12:10.326 --> 01:12:12.953
I think Homer G. Phillips
was chosen over City

01:12:13.037 --> 01:12:16.373
because the mayor felt it
was the easier thing to do.

01:12:16.498 --> 01:12:20.669
He would get less drawback
from the white community

01:12:20.753 --> 01:12:22.504
by closing Homer G. Phillips.

01:12:22.588 --> 01:12:24.298
- Well, Homer Phillips
was on the closing block

01:12:24.381 --> 01:12:25.966
from the time I came.

01:12:26.050 --> 01:12:28.886
Every year, it would
come that the hospital

01:12:29.011 --> 01:12:32.973
was going to be closed and by
the '70s,

01:12:34.224 --> 01:12:37.811
into the mid '70s, it had
become more of an issue

01:12:37.895 --> 01:12:40.689
that we were having meetings
with Washington University

01:12:40.773 --> 01:12:43.650
and they had told us that well,

01:12:43.734 --> 01:12:46.236
we are training more black
physicians than you are

01:12:46.362 --> 01:12:49.656
because most of your physicians
now are foreign born.

01:12:49.740 --> 01:12:50.657
- If you look at the list

01:12:50.741 --> 01:12:53.077
of where the residents came from
early on

01:12:53.202 --> 01:12:55.412
and compare that to a list
of where they came from

01:12:55.537 --> 01:12:58.207
much later in the life of the
hospital,

01:12:58.290 --> 01:13:00.834
you look down the list and
at the beginning it says,

01:13:00.918 --> 01:13:04.505
Meharry, Meharry,
Meharry, Meharry, Meharry.

01:13:04.588 --> 01:13:06.382
And if you look later on it's,
you know,

01:13:06.465 --> 01:13:08.550
Guatemala, it's Mexico.

01:13:08.634 --> 01:13:11.678
A lot of people were coming
from overseas at that point.

01:13:11.762 --> 01:13:13.680
Some people felt too that the
city

01:13:13.764 --> 01:13:15.682
had deliberately been pouring
resources

01:13:15.766 --> 01:13:19.103
into other hospitals,
the Caucasian hospitals,

01:13:19.228 --> 01:13:21.438
and that they had been
starving Homer G. Phillips

01:13:21.563 --> 01:13:23.107
of some of the new equipment,

01:13:23.232 --> 01:13:26.360
some of the technology that
they needed to do well.

01:13:26.443 --> 01:13:32.199
- Homer G. Phillips Hospital
had been a major flashpoint

01:13:33.617 --> 01:13:37.371
in the struggles of African
Americans in this community.

01:13:37.454 --> 01:13:41.250
In 1973-'74, there was the usual

01:13:42.084 --> 01:13:43.544
kind of community agitation

01:13:44.294 --> 01:13:45.963
to save the hospital.

01:13:46.088 --> 01:13:47.798
The major fight, the
biggest fight,

01:13:47.923 --> 01:13:51.593
was about electing a black
comptroller.

01:13:53.137 --> 01:13:55.931
And a black comptroller was
elected.

01:13:56.014 --> 01:13:57.766
His name was John Bass,

01:13:59.309 --> 01:14:00.561
and as a comptroller,

01:14:00.644 --> 01:14:03.981
he has the power and the
authority

01:14:04.106 --> 01:14:07.401
to determine what goes into the
budget.

01:14:07.484 --> 01:14:10.404
So because we had an
African American in there

01:14:10.487 --> 01:14:13.907
from 1973 to 1977

01:14:16.326 --> 01:14:18.829
Homer G. Phillips was
essentially safe.

01:14:18.954 --> 01:14:22.958
John Bass was defeated in March
of 1977.

01:14:24.126 --> 01:14:26.003
The new comptroller that came
in,

01:14:26.128 --> 01:14:28.839
his name was Raymond Percich.

01:14:28.964 --> 01:14:30.799
He came in on the promise

01:14:32.301 --> 01:14:34.511
along with the mayor of the city
then

01:14:34.636 --> 01:14:36.930
Mayor Conway, that they
were going to close

01:14:37.014 --> 01:14:38.432
Homer G. Phillips Hospital.

01:14:38.515 --> 01:14:40.684
- Everybody knows what that was
all about.

01:14:40.809 --> 01:14:43.812
The national health plan
was on the drawing board

01:14:43.896 --> 01:14:46.148
and everybody would be a paying
customer.

01:14:46.231 --> 01:14:48.192
It was the same way when I was a
kid.

01:14:48.317 --> 01:14:49.776
Before food stamps,

01:14:49.860 --> 01:14:52.321
there was Little Smile
Confectioneries

01:14:52.404 --> 01:14:54.615
all through our neighborhood.

01:14:54.698 --> 01:14:56.700
The big stores let you know

01:14:56.825 --> 01:14:59.036
if you don't have any
money, don't come here.

01:14:59.161 --> 01:15:02.164
The Little Confectionary says,
"We'll put it on the book

01:15:02.247 --> 01:15:04.625
and when you get some money
we'll sign."

01:15:05.375 --> 01:15:06.627
But when food stamps hit,

01:15:06.710 --> 01:15:09.463
immediately, the stores
that didn't welcome us

01:15:09.546 --> 01:15:11.840
put the big signs up.

01:15:11.924 --> 01:15:15.302
"Welcome food stamps,"
and as a result of it,

01:15:15.385 --> 01:15:18.305
a lot of the little
bitty stores shut down.

01:15:18.388 --> 01:15:20.891
Well, the same thing with
the national health plan.

01:15:21.016 --> 01:15:24.520
- In 1977 when all of us got
involved

01:15:24.603 --> 01:15:27.981
in the efforts to save
the comptroller's position

01:15:28.065 --> 01:15:30.359
and John Bass was defeated,

01:15:30.442 --> 01:15:32.861
the whole community from that
point on

01:15:32.945 --> 01:15:36.698
became an intense upheaval

01:15:37.574 --> 01:15:39.201
in order to save this hospital.

01:15:39.284 --> 01:15:45.374
- Every activist, you know,
from all corners, came together

01:15:45.457 --> 01:15:48.252
and so that's how, you know,

01:15:48.377 --> 01:15:49.878
I met Walle Amusa

01:15:49.962 --> 01:15:52.714
when he became involved in the
struggle

01:15:52.798 --> 01:15:54.758
to save Homer G. Phillips
Hospital.

01:15:54.883 --> 01:15:57.386
We formed an ad hoc committee

01:15:57.469 --> 01:15:59.429
to save Homer G. Phillips
Hospital

01:15:59.555 --> 01:16:01.348
and all public hospitals.

01:16:01.431 --> 01:16:04.101
- The radio disc jockey,

01:16:05.435 --> 01:16:06.853
as we were known at the time,
personality disc jockeys,

01:16:06.937 --> 01:16:10.023
were very involved in every
aspect of our community.

01:16:10.107 --> 01:16:11.692
Most of the people that I spoke
with

01:16:11.775 --> 01:16:15.362
around Homer G. Phillips
Hospital were ad hoc committees.

01:16:15.445 --> 01:16:20.033
They would form committees
themselves to save the hospital

01:16:20.117 --> 01:16:22.077
and they used my program as a
forum,

01:16:22.160 --> 01:16:24.538
as a voice to communicate to the
community

01:16:24.621 --> 01:16:25.872
and to tell them what was
happening,

01:16:25.956 --> 01:16:27.207
to tell them where the meetings
were,

01:16:27.291 --> 01:16:28.542
tell them why it was necessary

01:16:28.625 --> 01:16:29.876
for you to come to the meetings.

01:16:29.960 --> 01:16:32.379
If they wanted to keep
it open and they did.

01:16:32.462 --> 01:16:35.632
- We got involved

01:16:36.925 --> 01:16:40.721
when they were talking
about closing the hospitals.

01:16:41.805 --> 01:16:43.098
They were going to close both
hospitals,

01:16:43.181 --> 01:16:45.142
but they knew that the biggest
spike

01:16:45.267 --> 01:16:49.229
was gonna come from this
hospital.

01:16:49.313 --> 01:16:52.816
So the thing is don't

01:16:52.941 --> 01:16:55.402
close Number One first

01:16:55.485 --> 01:16:57.738
and allow the people at Homer
Phillips

01:16:57.821 --> 01:17:00.741
to dig in the trenches and
really give you a fight.

01:17:00.824 --> 01:17:03.827
Let's go at the big fight first

01:17:03.952 --> 01:17:06.496
and if we're able to get
Homer Phillips closed,

01:17:06.622 --> 01:17:08.415
when it comes time to close
Number One,

01:17:08.498 --> 01:17:12.461
we can close that hospital
at that snap of a finger.

01:17:13.503 --> 01:17:15.631
- [Walle] There were
demonstrations,

01:17:15.714 --> 01:17:18.967
there were picket signs,
there were petitions,

01:17:19.051 --> 01:17:22.596
there were sit ins in the
mayor's office.

01:17:22.679 --> 01:17:26.933
- Everywhere James Conway,
who was the mayor,

01:17:27.017 --> 01:17:30.103
would go for whatever event.

01:17:31.313 --> 01:17:33.273
Someone from the ad hoc
committee

01:17:33.357 --> 01:17:35.025
to save Homer G. Phillips
Hospital

01:17:35.150 --> 01:17:36.943
will be there to ask questions.

01:17:37.027 --> 01:17:41.114
We didn't care where, if it
was way South, we wound there

01:17:41.198 --> 01:17:44.451
and, yes, we became

01:17:44.534 --> 01:17:46.161
little shadows for him.

01:17:46.244 --> 01:17:47.621
- We went down to the mayor's
office

01:17:47.704 --> 01:17:49.623
where all of us got arrested,

01:17:49.706 --> 01:17:51.958
but the mayor tried to
come out the normal way,

01:17:52.042 --> 01:17:54.294
but we had the door blocked so,

01:17:54.378 --> 01:17:55.962
but what was so strange,

01:17:56.046 --> 01:17:59.299
he had these two huge
bodyguards on the outside

01:17:59.383 --> 01:18:01.635
(laughter)

01:18:01.718 --> 01:18:02.969
of the door.

01:18:03.053 --> 01:18:06.473
- And about nine o'clock
police came and said,

01:18:06.556 --> 01:18:07.849
you know, "You got to go.

01:18:07.933 --> 01:18:09.893
The building gotta be closed."

01:18:10.018 --> 01:18:12.020
They said, "No, we came to see
the mayor,

01:18:12.104 --> 01:18:15.315
and we gonna be here until he
sees us."

01:18:15.399 --> 01:18:18.485
So after a whole lot of
discussion, they said,

01:18:18.568 --> 01:18:21.029
"If you don't leave,
we gonna lock you up."

01:18:21.113 --> 01:18:22.989
So we said, "Lock us up."

01:18:23.073 --> 01:18:24.491
We were on the second level,

01:18:24.574 --> 01:18:27.536
they asked us to go
down to the first floor

01:18:27.619 --> 01:18:29.329
to get in a paddy wagon.

01:18:29.413 --> 01:18:30.664
We said no.

01:18:30.747 --> 01:18:33.875
- [Julia] They had to
carry us out on stretchers

01:18:33.959 --> 01:18:35.168
from the second floor.

01:18:35.252 --> 01:18:37.087
(laughter)

01:18:37.212 --> 01:18:39.840
- They were really tired when
they got finished with us.

01:18:39.923 --> 01:18:41.925
- It's funny now, but
it was not funny then

01:18:42.050 --> 01:18:44.010
because I think we were all
afraid.

01:18:44.094 --> 01:18:47.097
- The protests were organized

01:18:47.222 --> 01:18:49.266
and we marched through the
community.

01:18:49.391 --> 01:18:51.393
We marched downtown.

01:18:52.769 --> 01:18:54.855
We've marched everywhere that we
could

01:18:54.938 --> 01:18:56.440
to draw attention to the fact

01:18:56.565 --> 01:18:59.067
that we were losing this

01:18:59.151 --> 01:19:02.404
and we knew that if you lose the
hospital,

01:19:02.487 --> 01:19:04.197
you lost the community.

01:19:04.281 --> 01:19:07.033
(foreboding music)

01:19:10.120 --> 01:19:11.705
- It only took two votes

01:19:11.788 --> 01:19:13.874
on the Board of Estimate and
Apportionment

01:19:13.957 --> 01:19:17.794
in order to put money in the
budget

01:19:18.962 --> 01:19:22.048
to save this hospital, to keep
it running,

01:19:22.132 --> 01:19:26.136
and it didn't come to a
real flashpoint until 1978

01:19:27.471 --> 01:19:31.057
when the comptroller made a
promise

01:19:31.141 --> 01:19:33.602
that he will not put a
penny

01:19:34.311 --> 01:19:36.938
into the 1979 budget

01:19:37.939 --> 01:19:40.233
to operate this facility here.

01:19:40.317 --> 01:19:43.779
So essentially the hospital was
closed

01:19:43.862 --> 01:19:47.407
because we had an irresponsible,
racist,

01:19:47.491 --> 01:19:51.912
uncaring politician in the
name of Raymond Percich

01:19:51.995 --> 01:19:55.749
that colluded with the mayor to
get it.

01:19:55.832 --> 01:19:58.001
Two votes out of three

01:19:58.126 --> 01:20:03.089
to take money out of the
budget that would be used,

01:20:03.173 --> 01:20:06.259
you know that, to continue
to operate this facility.

01:20:06.343 --> 01:20:09.429
- The day that they closed the
hospital,

01:20:09.513 --> 01:20:12.307
I was at home, they had
told us all to take off

01:20:12.390 --> 01:20:16.436
and like I lived on the
backside of the hospital.

01:20:16.520 --> 01:20:18.480
So I heard helicopters, you
know,

01:20:18.563 --> 01:20:20.148
flying in the neighborhood
and I'm thinking,

01:20:20.232 --> 01:20:21.650
"What the devil is that?"

01:20:21.733 --> 01:20:23.610
and then when I looked across
the street,

01:20:23.693 --> 01:20:25.987
I saw all of these police cars.

01:20:26.071 --> 01:20:30.283
- I was at work as a
head nurse on One North.

01:20:31.409 --> 01:20:34.287
And they had stopped
hospital admissions now.

01:20:34.371 --> 01:20:38.166
Homer G. Phillips in the
African American community,

01:20:38.250 --> 01:20:41.336
the emergency room was
always accepting patients.

01:20:41.419 --> 01:20:44.130
So that was really strange

01:20:44.214 --> 01:20:46.466
and then if people became aware

01:20:46.550 --> 01:20:48.552
that they had brought moving
trucks,

01:20:48.677 --> 01:20:51.388
then I went to the switchboard

01:20:51.513 --> 01:20:54.724
to let folks know that they were
coming

01:20:54.850 --> 01:20:56.351
to close the hospital.

01:20:56.434 --> 01:20:59.729
And then everybody kinda
got in and alerting

01:20:59.855 --> 01:21:02.732
and notified their neighbors,

01:21:02.858 --> 01:21:05.735
people in the nearby community
were,

01:21:05.861 --> 01:21:07.237
you know, in the streets

01:21:07.362 --> 01:21:08.822
and folks that were coming to
work

01:21:08.905 --> 01:21:10.991
was finding out that the
hospital,

01:21:11.074 --> 01:21:12.367
you know, was closed.

01:21:12.450 --> 01:21:14.661
And they were transferring
patients

01:21:14.744 --> 01:21:17.664
and removing equipment and stuff

01:21:17.747 --> 01:21:23.253
from Homer G. Phillips and
that was August the 17th, 1979.

01:21:24.045 --> 01:21:26.006
- I was here at the day

01:21:27.215 --> 01:21:30.510
that the hospital was
actually forcibly closed.

01:21:31.511 --> 01:21:34.014
I had started coming my usual
way

01:21:34.097 --> 01:21:36.349
over Annie Malone Drive,

01:21:36.433 --> 01:21:38.268
which was a good avenue at that
time

01:21:38.393 --> 01:21:41.605
and I was met with the police.

01:21:41.730 --> 01:21:44.941
See, we did not have
cell phones at that time

01:21:45.066 --> 01:21:48.862
and the first act was to
cut off the switchboard.

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:54.367
So we could not make, no one
could make a call in or out.

01:21:54.451 --> 01:21:56.369
- [Walle] The community rose up
in arms,

01:21:56.453 --> 01:21:59.164
basically surrounded this
hospital.

01:22:04.628 --> 01:22:06.713
(knocking)

01:22:11.259 --> 01:22:13.553
- And I had never seen
anything like that before.

01:22:13.637 --> 01:22:17.766
It was just amazing that
they had that much

01:22:18.600 --> 01:22:20.268
police, you know, policemen

01:22:20.352 --> 01:22:22.562
that kind of police
power over there.

01:22:22.646 --> 01:22:25.106
I didn't know what they
were expecting or anything,

01:22:25.190 --> 01:22:27.400
but it was, it was something.

01:22:27.484 --> 01:22:30.695
- You had politicians, you had
ministers,

01:22:31.863 --> 01:22:34.824
you had students, you had young
people.

01:22:34.950 --> 01:22:36.910
Congressman Bill Clay, you know,

01:22:36.993 --> 01:22:40.956
his office was here every
day around the clock.

01:22:42.165 --> 01:22:44.250
Senator Gwen Giles,

01:22:45.293 --> 01:22:47.003
Marion Ross, Alderwoman Marion
Ross,

01:22:47.128 --> 01:22:48.922
Alderman Freeman Bosley.

01:22:50.131 --> 01:22:52.509
They were literally under siege
for this,

01:22:52.634 --> 01:22:56.429
surrounded this hospital
for almost 20 days.

01:22:56.513 --> 01:22:59.391
- People were quite beside
themselves

01:23:00.475 --> 01:23:02.018
and upset and emotional,

01:23:02.143 --> 01:23:07.315
you know, even as they tried
to take care of their patients

01:23:08.525 --> 01:23:12.821
The ones that were there,
and be normal or routine,

01:23:12.904 --> 01:23:15.281
but still the tension, you know,

01:23:15.365 --> 01:23:19.285
and the nervousness and
anxiety was shown by,

01:23:19.369 --> 01:23:23.498
you know, by all, I mean, some
people were actually crying.

01:23:23.581 --> 01:23:27.460
- It is important for the record
to show

01:23:27.544 --> 01:23:31.506
that this community did
not give up this hospital.

01:23:33.341 --> 01:23:34.509
It was stolen.

01:23:35.552 --> 01:23:37.303
- Their goal was to get in,

01:23:37.387 --> 01:23:39.556
sneak in, sneak attack, shut it
down,

01:23:39.681 --> 01:23:41.016
get everybody out of there

01:23:41.099 --> 01:23:43.685
and do what they had to do

01:23:43.768 --> 01:23:45.145
and that's the way they handled
it.

01:23:45.228 --> 01:23:48.815
Rather than telling the
public at a particular time,

01:23:48.898 --> 01:23:51.985
"We will be here and we
were going to shut it down,"

01:23:52.068 --> 01:23:55.739
and there would have
been a much larger crowd.

01:23:57.073 --> 01:24:00.201
Even the staff and people like
that,

01:24:00.285 --> 01:24:01.327
they didn't let them know,

01:24:01.411 --> 01:24:04.164
they found out the day
that people were coming

01:24:04.247 --> 01:24:05.999
to close, shut the doors down.

01:24:06.082 --> 01:24:10.545
- The closing of this hospital
was a military operation.

01:24:10.628 --> 01:24:12.714
It was 5 AM in the morning,

01:24:15.300 --> 01:24:16.843
helicopters,

01:24:18.219 --> 01:24:19.345
police dogs,

01:24:21.598 --> 01:24:23.641
police man on their horses.

01:24:25.477 --> 01:24:29.355
The state national guard put on
notice

01:24:29.439 --> 01:24:33.026
and it took that kind
of a military operation

01:24:38.740 --> 01:24:40.200
to snatch

01:24:42.327 --> 01:24:43.912
a jewel

01:24:47.791 --> 01:24:50.877
from the people in this
community.

01:24:52.962 --> 01:24:56.216
- Officially, the city might say

01:24:56.299 --> 01:25:00.970
that they closed Homer G.
Phillips on August the 19th,

01:25:01.096 --> 01:25:03.056
that's the, you know, the
official,

01:25:03.139 --> 01:25:05.600
that's just like
pronouncing somebody dead.

01:25:05.683 --> 01:25:07.060
They pronounced Homer G.
Phillips

01:25:07.143 --> 01:25:09.604
dead to them on August the 19th.

01:25:09.687 --> 01:25:14.234
Well, it was really August
the 17th to the community.

01:25:15.318 --> 01:25:17.737
- What you see here is just an
office.

01:25:19.155 --> 01:25:23.159
You say, what is the
significance of this office?

01:25:23.284 --> 01:25:29.124
This is the site of the new
Homer G.Phillips emergency room.

01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:34.504
It was built approximately 1977
to '78

01:25:35.672 --> 01:25:39.134
and the hospital didn't close in
1979.

01:25:40.343 --> 01:25:43.304
And many people said the
city just spent $2 million

01:25:43.388 --> 01:25:45.265
on a new the emergency room,

01:25:45.348 --> 01:25:48.768
they're certainly not going to
close Homer Phillips Hospital.

01:25:48.852 --> 01:25:52.438
And some of us said, if you
don't vote,

01:25:52.522 --> 01:25:54.315
if you don't participate,

01:25:54.399 --> 01:25:56.609
you won't get what you want.

01:25:56.693 --> 01:26:00.780
And indeed, the hospital
was closed in 1979.

01:26:02.115 --> 01:26:04.492
- To have the hospital closed
under the circumstances

01:26:04.576 --> 01:26:09.122
was just a horrible affront
to the

01:26:09.205 --> 01:26:13.042
consciousness of those who
really believed that their,

01:26:15.044 --> 01:26:19.674
their legacy as African
Americans was really based

01:26:19.757 --> 01:26:23.303
on that hospital and so to have
that close

01:26:23.386 --> 01:26:25.555
under cover of darkness

01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:27.473
and then to have a protest next
day

01:26:27.557 --> 01:26:29.517
only met with

01:26:31.728 --> 01:26:35.815
police with dogs
and the cars

01:26:37.066 --> 01:26:39.986
really to those African
Americans in St. Louis,

01:26:40.069 --> 01:26:44.199
it resembled the Bull
Connor days of Birmingham.

01:26:45.241 --> 01:26:47.202
And it just, it conjured those
images

01:26:47.285 --> 01:26:50.121
and it just really created a lot
of,

01:26:51.915 --> 01:26:54.334
a lot of apathy, a lot of anger

01:26:55.752 --> 01:26:58.922
which is really difficult to
discharge

01:26:59.047 --> 01:27:01.341
after all these years.

01:27:01.424 --> 01:27:03.343
Some of that's still there.

01:27:03.426 --> 01:27:05.178
- If you notice the people

01:27:05.261 --> 01:27:06.721
and the organizations that
closed

01:27:06.804 --> 01:27:08.264
Homer G. Phillips Hospital,

01:27:08.389 --> 01:27:10.350
they were not of St. Louis.

01:27:10.433 --> 01:27:12.685
It wasn't that important to
them.

01:27:12.769 --> 01:27:14.062
Like black radio.

01:27:14.145 --> 01:27:15.855
It's not important to some
people.

01:27:15.939 --> 01:27:18.691
It's like other black
institutions, like HBCUs.

01:27:18.775 --> 01:27:22.028
It's not important to those
who are outside of it.

01:27:22.111 --> 01:27:24.197
You have to be a part of it to
feel that,

01:27:24.280 --> 01:27:26.532
that love for it and to support
that love

01:27:26.616 --> 01:27:29.202
and support the structure
itself.

01:27:30.662 --> 01:27:34.040
If you just check out who closed
Homer G. Phillips Hospital,

01:27:34.123 --> 01:27:35.208
who was involved, and you'll see

01:27:35.291 --> 01:27:36.709
that they didn't care about,

01:27:36.793 --> 01:27:38.211
not only Homer G. Philips
Hospital,

01:27:38.294 --> 01:27:40.463
but African Americans in
general.

01:27:40.588 --> 01:27:44.050
- This is a sweatshirt that my
daughter

01:27:44.133 --> 01:27:46.886
who was seven to eight at the
time,

01:27:46.970 --> 01:27:49.889
around the struggle to
reopen the hospital,

01:27:49.973 --> 01:27:52.558
and she just adored this,

01:27:52.642 --> 01:27:55.103
and it says, "Vote yes, Homer G.
Phillips,

01:27:55.186 --> 01:27:58.690
"April the 7th, Campaign
for Human Dignity."

01:27:59.774 --> 01:28:03.319
And this is another

01:28:05.989 --> 01:28:08.074
prized treasure.

01:28:08.157 --> 01:28:12.495
"Reopen Homer G. Phillips,
Campaign for Human Dignity."

01:28:12.620 --> 01:28:16.958
- The fight to reopen the
hospital began immediate.

01:28:17.041 --> 01:28:19.836
The first level of action

01:28:20.628 --> 01:28:23.256
of course was to defeat

01:28:23.339 --> 01:28:25.967
the mayor

01:28:27.135 --> 01:28:29.762
who made the decision
to close the hospital.

01:28:29.846 --> 01:28:32.307
We led a major, you know,
campaign here

01:28:32.390 --> 01:28:34.475
to defeat Mayor Conway

01:28:34.559 --> 01:28:37.603
and another mayor was elected

01:28:37.687 --> 01:28:40.940
who promised to reopen the
hospital,

01:28:41.024 --> 01:28:43.568
that was Mayor Vincent
Schoemehl.

01:28:44.861 --> 01:28:48.656
He got in there, of course
he broke his promise.

01:28:52.160 --> 01:28:57.040
In the meantime, we had to
then resort to petitions.

01:28:58.708 --> 01:29:01.461
We had two petitions
that went on the ballot

01:29:01.544 --> 01:29:03.629
to reopen the hospital.

01:29:03.713 --> 01:29:09.135
Unfortunately, two of those
petitions got more than

01:29:11.179 --> 01:29:13.348
50%.

01:29:13.431 --> 01:29:16.684
The required percentage was 67%

01:29:17.977 --> 01:29:20.980
because South St. Louis,
which was predominantly white,

01:29:21.064 --> 01:29:25.193
voted overwhelmingly against
reopening this hospital

01:29:26.569 --> 01:29:30.573
but there were racial politics
plays in this country,

01:29:30.698 --> 01:29:32.575
where they used blacks against
whites,

01:29:32.700 --> 01:29:33.868
whites against blacks,

01:29:33.951 --> 01:29:36.204
and then by the time they get
through,

01:29:36.287 --> 01:29:38.331
most poor whites and poor blacks
are left

01:29:38.414 --> 01:29:40.166
to just fend for themselves.

01:29:40.249 --> 01:29:42.585
- People who knew the hospital
and knew the community

01:29:42.710 --> 01:29:45.338
and knew its importance
to the black community,

01:29:45.421 --> 01:29:50.093
worked hard to try to
keep it open to no avail,

01:29:51.469 --> 01:29:55.556
but it, a gallant fight to
make sure it would stay open.

01:29:55.640 --> 01:29:59.185
Then they came back
later to try to make sure

01:29:59.268 --> 01:30:01.354
it was not torn down
because there was talk

01:30:01.437 --> 01:30:03.689
about tearing the whole building
down.

01:30:03.773 --> 01:30:06.234
I think there's pride, the
fact that it's still here.

01:30:06.317 --> 01:30:10.196
- When I first arrive at the
site to accept the position

01:30:10.279 --> 01:30:13.449
it was interesting to
see that the hospital

01:30:13.574 --> 01:30:15.785
looked totally different
than what it looks now

01:30:15.910 --> 01:30:17.203
and that was in 2002,

01:30:17.286 --> 01:30:18.704
so that was the very first year

01:30:18.788 --> 01:30:21.874
that the facility opened for
residents.

01:30:21.958 --> 01:30:23.793
- I was looking for a place to
live.

01:30:23.918 --> 01:30:25.878
My husband was in the hospital

01:30:25.962 --> 01:30:28.214
and he was not going to survive
very long,

01:30:28.297 --> 01:30:31.634
I was told that, so I had to
make some changes in my life.

01:30:31.759 --> 01:30:35.054
So I began to downsize and I
looked around the community

01:30:35.138 --> 01:30:37.432
for senior citizens buildings

01:30:37.515 --> 01:30:39.767
and I got turned down a couple
of times

01:30:39.851 --> 01:30:41.394
and I turned some down.

01:30:41.477 --> 01:30:42.603
But when I came here,

01:30:42.687 --> 01:30:45.064
there was a gentleman in
the hall that said to me,

01:30:45.148 --> 01:30:49.902
"Excuse me, ma'am, is your
name Sandra Williams?"

01:30:49.986 --> 01:30:52.321
and Sheila who works in the
office here

01:30:52.447 --> 01:30:53.573
and my daughter said,

01:30:53.656 --> 01:30:55.992
"Oh, there's somebody who knows
you here."

01:30:56.117 --> 01:30:57.285
And yes he did.

01:30:57.368 --> 01:31:00.121
He remembered me because he
had been one of my supervisors

01:31:00.204 --> 01:31:02.582
in another health department.

01:31:02.665 --> 01:31:04.625
So I was like, "Well,
this must be the place

01:31:04.709 --> 01:31:06.586
that God has sent me to."

01:31:06.669 --> 01:31:09.755
- I needed a place with 24
security,

01:31:09.839 --> 01:31:12.508
you know, I needed a
place where on the inside

01:31:12.633 --> 01:31:14.677
I didn't wanna be by myself

01:31:15.803 --> 01:31:18.347
and someone told me about Homer
G.

01:31:19.849 --> 01:31:24.020
And I happened to come
and look around one day

01:31:24.145 --> 01:31:27.523
and I'm looking at the
amenities, not the people.

01:31:28.399 --> 01:31:29.609
They had a beauty salon there.

01:31:29.692 --> 01:31:31.277
I got my beauty shop license.

01:31:31.360 --> 01:31:34.489
They had barbershop, they
had the doctor's office,

01:31:34.572 --> 01:31:36.616
they had 24 security.

01:31:36.699 --> 01:31:37.825
They had a grocery store.

01:31:37.909 --> 01:31:40.119
If I didn't feel like
cooking, they had a cafe.

01:31:40.203 --> 01:31:42.497
I'm like, "Wow, this sounds
like the promised land

01:31:42.580 --> 01:31:43.831
that's in the Bible," you know,

01:31:43.915 --> 01:31:47.835
once you cross that joint and
go through all them problems,

01:31:47.919 --> 01:31:51.631
you know, the promised
land is there.

01:31:51.714 --> 01:31:54.175
And that's what I called
it when I came in.

01:31:54.258 --> 01:31:56.636
This is the promised land.

01:31:56.719 --> 01:32:01.516
- Personally, in order to
destroy a landmark like they did

01:32:02.683 --> 01:32:04.519
and then some others from out of
town,

01:32:04.602 --> 01:32:07.063
and out of state come in to
refurbish it

01:32:07.188 --> 01:32:09.148
and say this is a good thing

01:32:09.232 --> 01:32:12.235
for making living quarters for
profit,

01:32:13.569 --> 01:32:15.655
I thought it was disgraceful.

01:32:15.738 --> 01:32:19.158
I thought Homer G. Phillips
Hospital never should've closed,

01:32:19.242 --> 01:32:21.744
but had it reopened, I
think I should reopen

01:32:21.869 --> 01:32:23.996
partially as a hospital at
least.

01:32:24.080 --> 01:32:26.249
And for them to do what
they've done to it,

01:32:26.374 --> 01:32:29.085
although it's a beautiful
structure,

01:32:29.210 --> 01:32:31.420
I think it's disgraceful
personally.

01:32:31.546 --> 01:32:33.839
- [Nathaniel] I was impressed

01:32:33.923 --> 01:32:36.884
with what it had become to
be very honest with you.

01:32:36.968 --> 01:32:38.678
When I walked in that front
door,

01:32:38.761 --> 01:32:41.347
I didn't know it was Homer
Phillips.

01:32:41.430 --> 01:32:43.349
You know, it was gone.

01:32:43.432 --> 01:32:46.352
It's not Homer Phillips to me.

01:32:47.353 --> 01:32:48.938
- This building's stood as a
model

01:32:49.063 --> 01:32:50.690
certainly within the city of St.
Louis

01:32:50.773 --> 01:32:55.278
of what a senior living
for seniors could become

01:32:55.403 --> 01:32:57.029
and so it was the first time

01:32:57.113 --> 01:33:00.366
where we saw a full
service senior building.

01:33:00.449 --> 01:33:02.034
We set the standard for that
really here

01:33:02.118 --> 01:33:06.038
and so that is another
testament to Homer G. Phillips.

01:33:06.122 --> 01:33:10.126
Where we once again become
the symbol of excellence

01:33:10.251 --> 01:33:13.045
and how it is that we
look towards what this,

01:33:13.129 --> 01:33:14.422
how this should be done,

01:33:14.505 --> 01:33:16.591
and so I give a lot of credit
to the management company

01:33:16.674 --> 01:33:18.217
and to Miss Sharon Robnett,

01:33:18.301 --> 01:33:21.387
who was one of the African
American partners in this

01:33:21.470 --> 01:33:24.473
that had a vision for a
full service community.

01:33:24.599 --> 01:33:26.225
- When I look at the building
today,

01:33:26.309 --> 01:33:28.728
I think about what it was.

01:33:28.811 --> 01:33:33.232
You feel sad but you also feel
proud.

01:33:33.316 --> 01:33:34.942
It was a special point of your
life.

01:33:35.026 --> 01:33:39.155
You are able to experience
something you can share.

01:33:39.280 --> 01:33:41.657
I can talk about what was,

01:33:41.782 --> 01:33:44.076
which gives me hope of what
could be.

01:33:44.160 --> 01:33:48.456
But at the same time I realize
it's a special place in time

01:33:48.539 --> 01:33:51.250
that probably will never happen
again.

01:33:51.334 --> 01:33:55.755
Why is it that these
communities have reunions?

01:33:58.174 --> 01:34:00.509
As bad as they say they were,

01:34:00.635 --> 01:34:04.847
why is that people come back
each year for a reunion?

01:34:06.015 --> 01:34:08.434
Because it's a special place to
them

01:34:08.517 --> 01:34:11.270
and it has special meaning to
them.

01:34:17.360 --> 01:34:18.778
- In this case, there are side
mirrors,

01:34:18.861 --> 01:34:20.696
Homer G. Phillips nurses,

01:34:22.365 --> 01:34:25.618
a photograph of Sarah Jane
Banks.

01:34:25.701 --> 01:34:29.538
She was a wife of St.
Louis artist Spencer Banks,

01:34:29.664 --> 01:34:33.125
Ellen Bolton Brown, class of
1947.

01:34:34.210 --> 01:34:36.962
She was also last director of
nursing

01:34:37.046 --> 01:34:39.507
at Homer G. Phillips.

01:34:39.590 --> 01:34:44.220
Ms. Helen Pipes Blackwell, class
of 1942.

01:34:45.471 --> 01:34:49.392
She was also an instructor
at Homer G. Phillips.

01:34:49.517 --> 01:34:51.143
Mrs. Velma Jones,

01:34:52.228 --> 01:34:55.815
she was a nurse in the class of
1943,

01:34:57.066 --> 01:35:00.403
and also she was the first
African American nurse

01:35:00.528 --> 01:35:03.489
who worked at Barnes Hospital

01:35:03.572 --> 01:35:06.909
and she passed away 2010 at the
age of 91.

01:35:08.411 --> 01:35:11.539
And down here, Dr. Eugene
Mitchell,

01:35:11.622 --> 01:35:16.210
he was a director of medical
at Homer G. Phillips.

01:35:16.293 --> 01:35:18.879
He was also a graduate

01:35:18.963 --> 01:35:21.048
of the University of
Missouri Medical School,

01:35:21.132 --> 01:35:22.883
third African American to be
admitted

01:35:22.967 --> 01:35:25.344
and the first African American
to graduate

01:35:25.428 --> 01:35:27.888
from the University of Missouri.

01:35:27.972 --> 01:35:31.392
Okay, over here, you see various
artifacts

01:35:31.475 --> 01:35:34.478
of Homer G. Phillips Hospital
doctors,

01:35:35.730 --> 01:35:37.857
a table, a chair,

01:35:39.024 --> 01:35:41.777
here's a medical table
and a lot of photographs

01:35:41.902 --> 01:35:44.572
and newspaper clippings.

01:35:44.655 --> 01:35:46.866
Year books and equipment

01:35:46.949 --> 01:35:48.367
that was at Homer G. Phillips.

01:35:48.451 --> 01:35:52.371
Also have a couple of the capes.

01:35:53.414 --> 01:35:56.459
This cape belonged to Mrs. Velma
Jones,

01:35:56.584 --> 01:36:00.546
and the cap here belonged
to Miss Lula Hall.

01:36:00.629 --> 01:36:01.714
Here's another Cape that
belonged

01:36:01.797 --> 01:36:03.924
to a Miss Mary Lane Crawford,

01:36:04.008 --> 01:36:07.470
and then in the center
here, you got a uniform

01:36:07.595 --> 01:36:10.097
that also belonged to Velma
Jones

01:36:10.181 --> 01:36:12.349
when she was a nursing cadet

01:36:13.642 --> 01:36:16.395
that she, yeah, she graduated in
1945.

01:36:16.479 --> 01:36:18.731
- I was able to come over

01:36:18.814 --> 01:36:20.566
during the renovations

01:36:20.649 --> 01:36:24.069
and I was pleased, happy,

01:36:25.654 --> 01:36:27.448
delighted.

01:36:28.616 --> 01:36:31.786
Kind of fearful, not
knowing what I would find,

01:36:31.869 --> 01:36:36.290
wanted so very badly to go to
my division, my old division,

01:36:36.373 --> 01:36:39.126
which was pediatrics originally.

01:36:39.210 --> 01:36:42.797
I asked the gentleman if he
would take me up to fifth floor,

01:36:42.880 --> 01:36:44.632
which is the fifth floor of this
hospital

01:36:44.715 --> 01:36:47.927
on the south side, Five South,

01:36:48.010 --> 01:36:52.515
and the feeling that came
over me was of

01:36:53.432 --> 01:36:56.769
sadness. I almost wanted to cry

01:36:57.353 --> 01:36:59.271
as I feel now.

01:37:00.314 --> 01:37:02.817
And all that I could remember

01:37:04.026 --> 01:37:07.196
was the two wards which
extended east and west

01:37:07.321 --> 01:37:10.449
on the south side of the
building

01:37:10.533 --> 01:37:14.995
where we housed over 105
patients

01:37:15.079 --> 01:37:16.956
at one time.

01:37:17.039 --> 01:37:20.292
We would have patients
coming down the middle.

01:37:20.376 --> 01:37:23.295
They would be on either sides of
the wards

01:37:23.379 --> 01:37:26.799
in the heat when we did
not have air conditioning,

01:37:26.882 --> 01:37:31.053
and it came back to me that
we never lost a patient.

01:37:31.178 --> 01:37:34.974
Out of those 104 children
that we took care of

01:37:35.057 --> 01:37:39.895
that summer when it was such
a heat wave here in St. Louis,

01:37:40.020 --> 01:37:42.690
we didn't have adequate staff.

01:37:42.773 --> 01:37:47.236
But the skeleton staff that
we had on 7:00 to 3:30 shift,

01:37:47.361 --> 01:37:49.530
the 3:00 to 11:00 shift,

01:37:49.613 --> 01:37:53.659
and 11:00 to 7:00 shift
when we only had two nurses

01:37:53.742 --> 01:37:55.494
took care of those children.

01:37:55.578 --> 01:37:57.997
Those were the memories
that came back to me

01:37:58.080 --> 01:38:00.875
and I must have stayed up
there with the gentlemen

01:38:00.958 --> 01:38:02.710
for a good 20 minutes

01:38:02.793 --> 01:38:04.920
and he just asked that we move
along

01:38:05.045 --> 01:38:08.507
because he could see
that I was filling up,

01:38:08.591 --> 01:38:10.718
I can see it all over again.

01:38:11.886 --> 01:38:13.554
I love this place.

01:38:15.764 --> 01:38:17.975
It will never leave my memory

01:38:19.268 --> 01:38:22.021
and I'm so proud and so glad

01:38:22.104 --> 01:38:24.189
that those 300 and some dollars

01:38:24.273 --> 01:38:28.235
my mother and father
sacrificed to send me here

01:38:28.319 --> 01:38:31.739
and the one year that
I worked to help myself

01:38:31.822 --> 01:38:33.866
to come to nursing school.

01:38:33.949 --> 01:38:36.577
I am happy to say I am

01:38:37.620 --> 01:38:40.039
proud to be
a Homer Phillips nurse.

01:38:41.165 --> 01:38:44.209
Thank you very much,
I love coming in here.

01:38:44.293 --> 01:38:46.712
(approving murmurs)

01:38:46.795 --> 01:38:48.380
- She said it all.

01:38:49.840 --> 01:38:50.966
She said it all.

01:38:51.091 --> 01:38:53.135
(applause)

01:38:55.512 --> 01:38:57.139
- I'm from Tupelo, Mississippi,

01:38:57.264 --> 01:39:00.392
I spent my teen years here in
St. Louis

01:39:00.476 --> 01:39:02.227
before going away to college

01:39:02.311 --> 01:39:05.814
and then moved back here to
work at Washington University.

01:39:05.940 --> 01:39:08.484
I've been teaching the
Homer G. Phillips course,

01:39:08.609 --> 01:39:10.319
which is Homer G. Phillips:

01:39:10.444 --> 01:39:14.657
The Patient, Physician and
Community for five years.

01:39:14.782 --> 01:39:19.495
The class was developed probably
about 10 to 15 years prior

01:39:21.538 --> 01:39:23.916
because Washington University
realized

01:39:23.999 --> 01:39:26.251
that they had a number of
students,

01:39:26.335 --> 01:39:28.587
especially underrepresented
minorities,

01:39:28.671 --> 01:39:31.256
who were interested in health
when starting Wash. U.,

01:39:31.340 --> 01:39:35.094
but weren't applying in the
same percentages and numbers.

01:39:35.177 --> 01:39:36.595
- My family was very excited to
learn

01:39:36.679 --> 01:39:38.764
that I was taking a whole
course on Homer G. Phillips

01:39:38.847 --> 01:39:40.599
and not just visiting there for
one day

01:39:40.683 --> 01:39:42.142
because it meant a lot to them.

01:39:42.226 --> 01:39:43.602
A lot of us were like born
there.

01:39:43.686 --> 01:39:45.604
So there was a lot of family
connection

01:39:45.688 --> 01:39:47.272
and how much it meant to
the St. Louis community

01:39:47.356 --> 01:39:48.440
when they lived here still.

01:39:48.524 --> 01:39:50.776
They live mostly in
Springfield and Kansas now

01:39:50.859 --> 01:39:52.611
so not too much here in St.
Louis,

01:39:52.695 --> 01:39:55.447
but Homer G. was a driving
force in their community

01:39:55.531 --> 01:39:58.283
and to know that I'm in my
generation

01:39:58.367 --> 01:39:59.451
and still learning about it,

01:39:59.535 --> 01:40:01.453
it's still impacting our
family, meant a lot to them.

01:40:01.537 --> 01:40:02.955
- And so the first part of the
course,

01:40:03.038 --> 01:40:06.667
we go to The Ville, we figure
out what The Ville is about.

01:40:06.750 --> 01:40:08.627
We study the hospital.

01:40:08.711 --> 01:40:10.546
With that, I wanted to
kind of teach the lessons

01:40:10.671 --> 01:40:12.172
that come from Homer G. Phillips

01:40:12.256 --> 01:40:14.299
and that is the fact that the
physician

01:40:14.383 --> 01:40:16.468
is oftentimes more than the
physician.

01:40:16.552 --> 01:40:17.678
He's part of the community,

01:40:17.761 --> 01:40:19.638
she's part of the community,

01:40:19.722 --> 01:40:22.391
so she's not just taking care
of particular health needs,

01:40:22.516 --> 01:40:24.309
she's taking care of the whole
being.

01:40:24.393 --> 01:40:25.811
I think those are the principles

01:40:25.894 --> 01:40:28.480
that are important to
instill in the students.

01:40:28.564 --> 01:40:29.982
- No matter what I did after
college,

01:40:30.065 --> 01:40:31.692
I knew that I wanted to help
people.

01:40:31.775 --> 01:40:34.987
That was my main goal
and I think that taking,

01:40:35.070 --> 01:40:38.741
particularly taking the Homer G.
Course

01:40:38.866 --> 01:40:41.326
helped me solidify that
because it gave all of us

01:40:41.410 --> 01:40:46.040
an opportunity to see just the
different black professionals

01:40:46.123 --> 01:40:48.751
that have devoted their
lives to helping others

01:40:48.876 --> 01:40:50.169
and it also gave us an
opportunity

01:40:50.252 --> 01:40:53.589
to volunteer and work with
children

01:40:53.714 --> 01:40:55.174
at the children's hospital.

01:40:55.257 --> 01:40:58.427
It really helped me
see that helping people

01:40:58.552 --> 01:41:03.682
is when I felt like I was
doing something important,

01:41:04.391 --> 01:41:05.350
I guess I would say,

01:41:05.434 --> 01:41:08.437
it's kind of an understatement,
but yeah.

01:41:08.562 --> 01:41:10.355
- We want our students,

01:41:10.439 --> 01:41:12.524
if they really want to help
people

01:41:12.608 --> 01:41:13.901
and they really believe that
health care

01:41:13.984 --> 01:41:16.445
is that route that we help them

01:41:16.570 --> 01:41:18.864
and equip them with the tools

01:41:18.947 --> 01:41:21.033
but we can't do that without
community.

01:41:21.116 --> 01:41:23.619
Our physicians couldn't
do that without community

01:41:23.744 --> 01:41:25.537
when they were at Homer G.
Phillips

01:41:25.621 --> 01:41:28.707
and then they can't do it
out the community as well.

01:41:28.791 --> 01:41:33.045
- Homer G. Phillips is just
it's really

01:41:35.297 --> 01:41:37.883
a major point

01:41:37.966 --> 01:41:41.929
that highlights a bigger

01:41:42.012 --> 01:41:45.808
dynamic here in St. Louis.

01:41:45.933 --> 01:41:48.060
And the impact that
we've had on the nation,

01:41:48.143 --> 01:41:52.648
particularly with regards to
the civil rights movement.

01:41:52.773 --> 01:41:55.901
Most of your major civil rights

01:41:57.194 --> 01:42:01.740
and legal components started
right here in St. Louis

01:42:02.699 --> 01:42:05.077
going back to the Dred Scott
Case

01:42:05.160 --> 01:42:07.246
and it goes on and on and on.

01:42:07.329 --> 01:42:09.623
The Urban League was developed
as a result

01:42:09.706 --> 01:42:11.792
of what took place here in St.
Louis,

01:42:11.875 --> 01:42:14.336
all the way up to the present

01:42:14.461 --> 01:42:17.756
with the Ferguson situation.

01:42:19.007 --> 01:42:26.640
And so this speaks volumes
as to the rich culture

01:42:27.641 --> 01:42:29.518
that has evolved here in St.
Louis,

01:42:29.643 --> 01:42:31.854
and that can continue to grow.

01:42:33.147 --> 01:42:35.649
And I believe that this is gonna
certainly

01:42:35.732 --> 01:42:38.694
put a fire in the community

01:42:39.444 --> 01:42:40.821
to really take a passion

01:42:40.904 --> 01:42:43.991
in what we've accomplished over
the years.

01:42:44.074 --> 01:42:47.202
- It's literally a who's who
among St. Louis titans, right?

01:42:47.327 --> 01:42:50.873
And so, you know, we have
people from Dick Gregory

01:42:50.998 --> 01:42:55.878
and Tina Turner and so everybody
from every walk of life,

01:42:57.337 --> 01:43:00.841
Grace Bumbry, like, it's just
an amazing place to be at.

01:43:00.924 --> 01:43:05.179
I heard from an alumni,
the actual Chuck Berry

01:43:05.262 --> 01:43:06.972
performed in this very room.

01:43:07.055 --> 01:43:08.640
It used to be the auditorium

01:43:08.724 --> 01:43:10.184
and now it's our library.

01:43:10.267 --> 01:43:13.353
But you just see those
sorts of things all the time

01:43:13.437 --> 01:43:15.856
with our school and who we are,

01:43:15.939 --> 01:43:18.317
we're working tirelessly

01:43:18.400 --> 01:43:20.694
to get back to that point and
even extend

01:43:20.777 --> 01:43:24.448
and so that's what we're
really excited about.

01:43:26.074 --> 01:43:27.492
- My name is Becky

01:43:27.576 --> 01:43:30.871
and my father is Dr. Earle U.
Robinson Jr.

01:43:31.955 --> 01:43:33.749
Ever since I was a little girl,

01:43:33.874 --> 01:43:36.710
I remember my father
always telling stories

01:43:36.793 --> 01:43:38.712
about when he was a little boy,

01:43:38.795 --> 01:43:42.049
when he was living in Atlantic
City

01:43:42.132 --> 01:43:43.675
when he went off to college,

01:43:43.759 --> 01:43:47.012
when he went to medical
school, ups and downs.

01:43:47.095 --> 01:43:48.388
There's so many things.

01:43:48.472 --> 01:43:52.184
My father has just been such
an integral part of my life

01:43:52.267 --> 01:43:53.936
and he's taught me so much

01:43:54.061 --> 01:43:58.190
and that's probably why I am
the person that I am today

01:43:58.273 --> 01:44:02.527
is because of him and his
philosophies and things like
that.

01:44:02.611 --> 01:44:05.906
But at this time in his life,

01:44:05.989 --> 01:44:08.700
I know that he's

01:44:09.409 --> 01:44:10.786
getting to the age

01:44:10.911 --> 01:44:14.289
where his health is a factor

01:44:14.957 --> 01:44:16.250
and I,

01:44:16.333 --> 01:44:19.795
I really want other
people to be able to know

01:44:19.920 --> 01:44:21.588
more about my father

01:44:21.672 --> 01:44:26.260
and how he played an
important role in medicine.

01:44:27.219 --> 01:44:30.389
Just as a person and as a
father.

01:44:30.472 --> 01:44:32.057
One thing that really touched me

01:44:32.140 --> 01:44:36.979
is when I finally drove
up to the hospital.

01:44:37.980 --> 01:44:40.732
This is my first time in St.
Louis

01:44:40.816 --> 01:44:44.736
and I live in Indianapolis
and it's only, you know,

01:44:44.820 --> 01:44:46.822
a four hour drive and
it was just interesting

01:44:46.947 --> 01:44:50.075
that I had never been in St.
Louis before

01:44:50.158 --> 01:44:52.244
and I'm coming down that street

01:44:52.327 --> 01:44:53.912
and pulling up to the hospital.

01:44:53.996 --> 01:44:55.831
I've seen all these pictures

01:44:55.956 --> 01:44:59.084
and I didn't know what I would
feel

01:44:59.167 --> 01:45:00.919
when I first got here.

01:45:01.003 --> 01:45:05.799
But I felt a sense that
it was the right timing.

01:45:07.175 --> 01:45:10.804
It was meant to be and I'm
doing something super amazing

01:45:11.638 --> 01:45:12.306
for not just my grandfather,

01:45:13.181 --> 01:45:15.934
but for my father and my family.

01:45:17.352 --> 01:45:19.062
Within the entrance,

01:45:19.187 --> 01:45:21.106
I saw this picture years ago,

01:45:21.189 --> 01:45:24.484
my grandfather was standing
next to this stairway

01:45:24.568 --> 01:45:28.030
and I didn't know exactly where
it was

01:45:28.155 --> 01:45:30.615
and I assumed it was, you know,

01:45:30.699 --> 01:45:32.784
the entrance of the Homer
G. Phillips Hospital.

01:45:32.868 --> 01:45:37.789
But you know, now that it's
a retirement living home,

01:45:37.873 --> 01:45:41.543
I walked in, was shown
that it was the steps

01:45:42.210 --> 01:45:42.544
that my father,

01:45:43.670 --> 01:45:47.549
my grandfather originally stood
next to

01:45:47.674 --> 01:45:49.217
and the one thing that I wanted
to do

01:45:49.343 --> 01:45:52.387
was stand right there and be
able to be

01:45:52.512 --> 01:45:54.890
in the same place that
my grandfather stood

01:45:55.015 --> 01:45:58.226
and I told Joyce, I said,
"Please take a picture.

01:45:58.352 --> 01:46:00.979
I have to send this to
my father right away,"

01:46:01.063 --> 01:46:05.317
and I called my father
later on that afternoon

01:46:06.526 --> 01:46:08.653
to let him know that I
walked in the hospital,

01:46:08.737 --> 01:46:10.739
that I got to stand
right there at the steps

01:46:10.864 --> 01:46:14.368
and the first thing my father
said,

01:46:14.451 --> 01:46:17.996
"Your grandfather is extremely
proud,"

01:46:18.997 --> 01:46:21.041
and I just, you know, let him
know

01:46:21.124 --> 01:46:24.044
that it's a dream come true.

01:46:24.878 --> 01:46:25.754
Originally, my father and I,

01:46:25.837 --> 01:46:27.881
we discussed about doing this
project

01:46:27.964 --> 01:46:30.842
and he wasn't sure if it was
gonna happen,

01:46:30.926 --> 01:46:34.554
but I think every day
that I see that this team

01:46:34.638 --> 01:46:37.891
is working so hard to make this
happen,

01:46:37.974 --> 01:46:41.520
this film is happening and I'm
just,

01:46:41.603 --> 01:46:45.857
I'm just grateful and I'm
just really passionate

01:46:45.941 --> 01:46:49.569
about what I do and what I put
my mind to

01:46:49.653 --> 01:46:51.696
and I'm just so thankful.

01:46:51.780 --> 01:46:53.031
I really am.

